/* Content ported from fascia-body-flow (Player 1's own app): Performance Hub data, clinical domain data, fascia routines */
const FBF = {"anatomyData":{"bodyParts":[{"id":"head","name":"Head & Brain","region":"upper","systems":{"muscle":"Facial muscles control expression, mastication, and eye movement. The temporalis and masseter are among the strongest muscles relative to their size.","tendon":"The temporoparietal fascia and galea aponeurotica cover the skull. The temporomandibular joint (TMJ) tendon controls jaw movement.","nerve":"The brain and 12 pairs of cranial nerves control everything from vision and smell to facial sensation and tongue movement. The trigeminal nerve is the largest.","bone":"The skull consists of the cranium (8 bones) and facial bones (14 bones). The mandible is the only freely movable bone in the skull.","fascia":"The cranial fascia and galea aponeurotica form a continuous fascial sheet connecting the neck fascia to the forehead — tension here can cause headaches."},"injuries":[{"name":"Concussion","recovery":"3-4 months (mild) to 6-24+ months (severe)","description":"Mild traumatic brain injury from impact. Requires rest, gradual return to activity."},{"name":"Skull Fracture","recovery":"2-3 months","description":"Break in the cranial bone from direct trauma."},{"name":"TMJ Dysfunction","recovery":"1-3 months","description":"Jaw joint pain and limited movement, often linked to fascial tension."}]},{"id":"neck","name":"Neck & Cervical Spine","region":"upper","systems":{"muscle":"The sternocleidomastoid, trapezius, and deep cervical flexors control head position. The suboccipital muscles at the base of the skull are rich in proprioceptors.","tendon":"The nuchal ligament runs from the skull to the spine, providing elastic support. Deep cervical ligaments stabilize each vertebra.","nerve":"The cervical plexus and brachial plexus originate here. The phrenic nerve controls breathing. Spinal cord passes through the vertebral foramen.","bone":"Seven cervical vertebrae (C1-C7). C1 (atlas) and C2 (axis) allow the head to nod and rotate. The hyoid bone anchors the tongue.","fascia":"The cervical fascia is divided into four layers: superficial, investing, pretracheal, and prevertebral — forming a continuous sleeve that connects to the thoracic fascia."},"injuries":[{"name":"Whiplash","recovery":"1-3 months","description":"Neck strain from sudden acceleration/deceleration. Common in sports and car accidents."},{"name":"Cervical Disc Herniation","recovery":"3-6+ months","description":"Disc material presses on nerves, causing arm pain and numbness."},{"name":"Stinger/Burner","recovery":"Minutes to weeks","description":"Stretching of the brachial plexus causing temporary arm weakness or burning sensation."}]},{"id":"shoulder","name":"Shoulders","region":"upper","systems":{"muscle":"The rotator cuff (supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, subscapularis) stabilizes the joint. The deltoid, pectoralis, and latissimus provide power.","tendon":"The rotator cuff tendons fuse with the joint capsule. The long head of biceps tendon passes through the groove. The supraspinatus tendon is the most commonly torn.","nerve":"The brachial plexus (C5-T1) branches into the axillary, musculocutaneous, radial, median, and ulnar nerves — supplying the entire arm.","bone":"The shoulder girdle includes the scapula, clavicle, and humerus. The glenohumeral joint is the most mobile joint in the body — and the least stable.","fascia":"The thoracolumbar fascia connects the shoulders to the opposite hip via the posterior oblique chain. The axillary fascia forms a sleeve around the arm."},"injuries":[{"name":"Rotator Cuff Tear","recovery":"4-12+ months","description":"Tearing of the supraspinatus or other cuff tendons. Grade 1-3 severity."},{"name":"Shoulder Dislocation","recovery":"2-6 months","description":"Humeral head pops out of the socket. High recurrence rate without rehab."},{"name":"Labral Tear (SLAP)","recovery":"6-12+ months","description":"Tear of the cartilage ring stabilizing the shoulder joint."}]},{"id":"chest","name":"Chest & Thoracic Spine","region":"core","systems":{"muscle":"The pectoralis major, intercostals, and serratus anterior power pushing and breathing. The diaphragm is the primary breathing muscle and a key fascial attachment.","tendon":"Costovertebral ligaments connect ribs to vertebrae. The sternoclavicular joint is the only bony connection between the arm and the trunk.","nerve":"Intercostal nerves run between ribs. The vagus nerve passes through the thorax. The cardiac plexus controls the heart.","bone":"12 thoracic vertebrae, 12 pairs of ribs, and the sternum form the rib cage — protecting the heart and lungs while enabling breathing.","fascia":"The endothoracic fascia lines the chest cavity. The pectoral fascia connects to the abdominal fascia and the arm fascia through the axillary region."},"injuries":[{"name":"Rib Fracture","recovery":"5-7 weeks","description":"Broken rib from direct impact or coughing. Painful but usually heals without intervention."},{"name":"Costochondritis","recovery":"1-3 months","description":"Inflammation of the cartilage connecting ribs to the sternum."},{"name":"Thoracic Disc Herniation","recovery":"3-6 months","description":"Less common than lumbar but can cause back pain and neurological symptoms."}]},{"id":"core","name":"Core & Lumbar Spine","region":"core","systems":{"muscle":"The rectus abdominis, transverse abdominis, obliques, and quadratus lumborum form the core. The multifidus is the deepest spinal stabilizer — weakness predicts back pain.","tendon":"The thoracolumbar fascia is the largest tendon sheet in the body, connecting the back, glutes, and hamstrings. The linea alba is the central abdominal tendon.","nerve":"The lumbar plexus (T12-L4) gives rise to the femoral, obturator, and lateral femoral cutaneous nerves. The sciatic nerve forms from L4-S3.","bone":"Five lumbar vertebrae (L1-L5) carry the most weight. The L5-S1 disc is the most commonly herniated. The sacrum connects to the pelvis.","fascia":"The thoracolumbar fascia is the critical link — it transmits force from the hips to the shoulders via the posterior oblique chain (latissimus to opposite glute)."},"injuries":[{"name":"Lumbar Disc Herniation","recovery":"3-6+ months","description":"Disc material presses on the sciatic nerve, causing leg pain (sciatica)."},{"name":"Muscle Strain","recovery":"1-4 weeks","description":"Tearing of the back muscles. Grade 1 (mild) to Grade 3 (complete tear)."},{"name":"Spondylolysis","recovery":"3-6 months","description":"Stress fracture of the vertebra — common in young athletes with repetitive extension."}]},{"id":"hips","name":"Hips & Pelvis","region":"lower","systems":{"muscle":"The gluteus maximus is the largest muscle in the body. The psoas connects the spine to the femur. The deep rotators (piriformis, gemelli) control hip rotation.","tendon":"The iliotibial band (ITB) is a thick fascial band running from the hip to the knee. The gluteal tendons attach to the greater trochanter.","nerve":"The sciatic nerve passes under (or sometimes through) the piriformis. The pudendal nerve supplies the pelvic floor. The femoral nerve powers the quads.","bone":"The pelvis (ilium, ischium, pubis), sacrum, and femur form the hip joint. The acetabulum is the socket; the femoral head is the ball.","fascia":"The hip capsule and iliotibial band are critical fascial structures. The lateral line runs from the hip to the ankle. The deep front line passes through the pelvis."},"injuries":[{"name":"Hip Labral Tear","recovery":"6-12+ months","description":"Tear of the cartilage ring around the hip socket. Common in rotation sports."},{"name":"Piriformis Syndrome","recovery":"1-3 months","description":"The piriformis compresses the sciatic nerve, mimicking disc pain."},{"name":"Hip Flexor Strain","recovery":"2-6 weeks","description":"Tearing of the iliopsoas or rectus femoris. Common in sprinting and kicking."}]},{"id":"upperArm","name":"Upper Arm","region":"upper","systems":{"muscle":"The biceps brachii flexes the elbow and supinates the forearm. The triceps extends the elbow. The brachialis is the primary elbow flexor.","tendon":"The long head of biceps tendon originates at the shoulder and is prone to tendinopathy. The triceps tendon attaches to the olecranon.","nerve":"The musculocutaneous nerve supplies the biceps and brachialis. The radial nerve supplies the triceps and wraps around the humerus in the spiral groove.","bone":"The humerus is the upper arm bone. The surgical neck is a common fracture site. The radial groove is where the radial nerve passes.","fascia":"The brachial fascia forms a sleeve around the arm, continuous with the axillary fascia above and the antebrachial fascia below."},"injuries":[{"name":"Biceps Tendinopathy","recovery":"2-6 weeks","description":"Inflammation or degeneration of the biceps tendon."},{"name":"Triceps Strain","recovery":"1-4 weeks","description":"Tearing of the triceps muscle, usually from explosive extension."},{"name":"Humerus Fracture","recovery":"5-12 weeks","description":"Break of the upper arm bone from direct impact or fall."}]},{"id":"elbow","name":"Elbow & Forearm","region":"upper","systems":{"muscle":"The forearm flexors (wrist and finger flexors) and extensors control grip and wrist movement. The pronator teres and supinator control forearm rotation.","tendon":"The common flexor tendon (medial epicondyle) and common extensor tendon (lateral epicondyle) are the attachment points for all forearm muscles.","nerve":"The median nerve passes through the carpal tunnel. The ulnar nerve passes through the cubital tunnel (your 'funny bone'). The radial nerve supplies the extensors.","bone":"The humerus, radius, and ulna form the elbow joint. The olecranon is the point of the elbow. The radial head rotates during pronation/supination.","fascia":"The antebrachial fascia covers the forearm. The interosseous membrane between the radius and ulna is a key fascial structure that transmits force."},"injuries":[{"name":"Tennis Elbow","recovery":"3-9 months","description":"Lateral epicondylitis — degeneration of the extensor tendon. Chronic cases are slow to heal."},{"name":"Golfer's Elbow","recovery":"3-6 months","description":"Medial epicondylitis — degeneration of the flexor tendon."},{"name":"UCL Tear","recovery":"12-24+ months","description":"Ulnar collateral ligament tear — career-threatening for throwing athletes."}]},{"id":"wrist","name":"Wrist & Hand","region":"upper","systems":{"muscle":"The thenar and hypothenar muscles control the thumb and little finger. The lumbricals and interossei provide fine finger control. Grip strength is a biomarker for longevity.","tendon":"The flexor tendons pass through the carpal tunnel. The extensor tendons cross the back of the wrist. The extensor hood covers the fingers.","nerve":"The median nerve (thumb, index, middle), ulnar nerve (ring, little finger), and radial nerve (back of hand) supply sensation and motor control.","bone":"Eight carpal bones form the wrist. Five metacarpals form the palm. Fourteen phalanges form the fingers. The scaphoid is the most commonly fractured carpal.","fascia":"The palmar aponeurosis is a thick fascial sheet in the palm. The extensor retinaculum holds the extensor tendons in place. Fascial connections run to the forearm."},"injuries":[{"name":"Carpal Tunnel Syndrome","recovery":"1-6 months","description":"Compression of the median nerve in the wrist. Night pain and numbness."},{"name":"Scaphoid Fracture","recovery":"10-12 months","description":"Fracture of the scaphoid bone — poor blood supply makes healing slow."},{"name":"TFCC Tear","recovery":"6-12+ months","description":"Triangular fibrocartilage complex tear on the pinky side of the wrist."}]},{"id":"thigh","name":"Thigh","region":"lower","systems":{"muscle":"The quadriceps (rectus femoris, vasti) extend the knee. The hamstrings (biceps femoris, semitendinosus, semimembranosus) flex the knee and extend the hip. The adductors pull the legs together.","tendon":"The quadriceps tendon attaches to the patella, which connects to the tibia via the patellar tendon. The hamstring tendons attach to the ischial tuberosity (sit bone).","nerve":"The femoral nerve powers the quads. The sciatic nerve branches into the tibial and common peroneal nerves at the knee. The obturator nerve supplies the adductors.","bone":"The femur is the longest and strongest bone in the body. The greater trochanter is the hip's outer point. The patella is the largest sesamoid bone.","fascia":"The fascia lata covers the thigh. The iliotibial band is the lateral thickening. The adductor canal transmits vessels and nerves. Fascial connections run to the knee."},"injuries":[{"name":"Hamstring Strain","recovery":"2-8 weeks","description":"The most common injury in sprinting sports. High recurrence rate without proper rehab."},{"name":"Quadriceps Strain","recovery":"1-6 weeks","description":"Tearing of the quadriceps, usually the rectus femoris."},{"name":"IT Band Syndrome","recovery":"1-3 months","description":"Friction of the iliotibial band over the lateral knee — common in runners."}]},{"id":"knee","name":"Knee","region":"lower","systems":{"muscle":"The quadriceps extend the knee. The hamstrings flex it. The popliteus unlocks the knee from full extension. The gastrocnemius crosses the back of the knee.","tendon":"The ACL prevents anterior tibial translation. The PCL prevents posterior translation. The MCL and LCL prevent side-to-side movement. The patellar tendon transmits quad force.","nerve":"The tibial nerve supplies the calf. The common peroneal nerve wraps around the fibular head — vulnerable to compression. The saphenous nerve provides sensation.","bone":"The femur, tibia, patella, and fibula form the knee. The tibial plateau is the upper surface. The menisci are C-shaped cartilage that absorb shock and improve fit.","fascia":"The knee capsule and retinacula are key fascial structures. The iliotibial band inserts at the knee. The pes anserinus is where three tendons meet on the inner knee."},"injuries":[{"name":"ACL Tear","recovery":"9-12 months","description":"The most devastating sports injury. Reconstruction takes 9+ months. Grafts take 12-24+ months."},{"name":"Meniscus Tear","recovery":"6-12+ months","description":"Cartilage tear — repair is slow, removal leads to arthritis."},{"name":"Patellar Tendinopathy","recovery":"3-9 months","description":"'Jumper's knee' — degeneration of the patellar tendon from repetitive jumping."}]},{"id":"lowerLeg","name":"Lower Leg","region":"lower","systems":{"muscle":"The gastrocnemius and soleus (calf) plantarflex the foot. The tibialis anterior dorsiflexes it. The peroneals (fibularis) evert the foot.","tendon":"The Achilles tendon is the largest and strongest tendon in the body. The posterior tibialis tendon supports the arch. The peroneal tendons stabilize the outer ankle.","nerve":"The tibial nerve supplies the calf. The deep peroneal nerve supplies the shin muscles. The sural nerve provides sensation to the outer calf.","bone":"The tibia is the main weight-bearing bone. The fibula provides lateral stability. The tibia is the most common site of stress fractures in athletes.","fascia":"The crural fascia (deep fascia of the leg) forms a tight sleeve. Compartment syndrome occurs when pressure builds within these fascial compartments."},"injuries":[{"name":"Achilles Tendinopathy","recovery":"3-9 months","description":"Degeneration of the Achilles tendon. Chronic cases are very slow to heal."},{"name":"Tibial Stress Fracture","recovery":"4-8 weeks","description":"Hairline crack in the tibia from repetitive impact. Common in runners."},{"name":"Shin Splints","recovery":"2-6 weeks","description":"Medial tibial stress syndrome — inflammation of the muscles and fascia along the shin."}]},{"id":"ankle","name":"Ankle & Foot","region":"lower","systems":{"muscle":"The intrinsic foot muscles (abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum) support the arch. The tibialis posterior is the primary arch support. Strong feet are the foundation of athletic performance.","tendon":"The Achilles tendon inserts at the heel. The posterior tibialis tendon supports the arch. The peroneal tendons run behind the lateral malleolus.","nerve":"The tibial nerve branches into medial and lateral plantar nerves. The deep peroneal nerve supplies the web space between the first and second toes.","bone":"26 bones in each foot: 7 tarsals, 5 metatarsals, 14 phalanges. The talus forms the ankle joint. The calcaneus is the heel bone. The arch is supported by bone and ligament.","fascia":"The plantar fascia is the thick band on the bottom of the foot — from heel to toes. It acts as a spring during walking and running. The spring ligament supports the arch."},"injuries":[{"name":"Ankle Sprain","recovery":"2-8 weeks","description":"Stretching or tearing of the lateral ligaments. Grade 1 (mild) to Grade 3 (complete tear)."},{"name":"Plantar Fasciitis","recovery":"3-9 months","description":"Inflammation of the plantar fascia. Morning pain is the hallmark symptom."},{"name":"Achilles Rupture","recovery":"6-12+ months","description":"Complete tear of the Achilles tendon. Often requires surgery."}]}],"doseComponents":[{"name":"Magnitude","description":"How much force is applied"},{"name":"Duration","description":"How long the force lasts"},{"name":"Rate","description":"How quickly force is applied"},{"name":"Cycles","description":"How many repetitions occur"},{"name":"Direction","description":"Where the force is directed"},{"name":"Distribution","description":"How the force spreads across tissue"}],"healingFactors":[{"icon":"AlertTriangle","title":"Severity","text":"The worse the injury, the longer it takes to heal. Trust the process!"},{"icon":"Droplets","title":"Hydration","text":"Dehydrated tissues heal slower & can get injured more easily."},{"icon":"Utensils","title":"Nutrition","text":"Protein needs increase during healing so make sure to fuel your body."},{"icon":"Moon","title":"Sleep","text":"Sleep is essential for tissue/cell repair & inflammation control."},{"icon":"Dumbbell","title":"Rehab","text":"Appropriate loading & exercise is important for tissue healing when appropriate."}],"loadTypes":[{"id":"tension","name":"Tension","action":"Pulls Apart","color":"#f7e06a","tissues":["Muscle","Tendon","Ligament"],"movements":["Sprint","Jump","Heavy Lift"]},{"id":"compression","name":"Compression","action":"Presses Together","color":"#d95e68","tissues":["Cartilage","Bone","Disc"],"movements":["Squat","Land","Carry"]},{"id":"shear","name":"Shear","action":"Slides Sideways","color":"#51c9d6","tissues":["Cartilage","Meniscus","Disc"],"movements":["Cut","Brake","Pivot"]},{"id":"bending","name":"Bending","action":"Curves Under Load","color":"#a361ea","tissues":["Long Bone","Vertebrae"],"movements":["Run","Jump","Throw"]},{"id":"torsion","name":"Torsion","action":"Twists Around Axis","color":"#45b787","tissues":["Bone","Disc","Ligament"],"movements":["Cut","Throw","Kick"]}],"performanceDomains":[{"title":"Strength","icon":"Dumbbell","description":"The ability to produce force. Maximal strength is the foundation of all athletic qualities. Train through full ranges of motion to load fascia in all planes."},{"title":"Power","icon":"Zap","description":"Force × velocity. The ability to produce force quickly. Plyometric training loads the fascial system through rapid stretch-shortening cycles."},{"title":"Speed","icon":"Gauge","description":"The ability to move quickly. Sprinting places the highest loads on the hamstrings and Achilles tendon — the two most injury-prone tissues."},{"title":"Mobility","icon":"Move","description":"Active range of motion. Not the same as flexibility — mobility requires strength at end ranges. Fascia restricts or enables movement based on hydration and tension."},{"title":"Endurance","icon":"Heart","description":"The ability to sustain effort. Aerobic fitness improves recovery between efforts and between training sessions. Fascial health depends on regular movement."},{"title":"Recovery","icon":"Moon","description":"The other side of training. Tissues adapt during recovery, not during training. Sleep, nutrition, hydration, and active recovery are non-negotiable."}],"timelineColumns":[{"label":"1-2 WKS","start":1,"end":2},{"label":"3-4 WKS","start":3,"end":4},{"label":"5-7 WKS","start":5,"end":7},{"label":"2-3 MO","start":8,"end":12},{"label":"4-6 MO","start":16,"end":24},{"label":"7-9 MO","start":28,"end":36},{"label":"10-12 MO","start":40,"end":48},{"label":"1-2 YRS","start":52,"end":104},{"label":"MAY NOT HEAL","start":104,"end":110}],"tissueHealingData":[{"tissue":"Muscle","color":"#d95e68","ranges":[{"label":"Grade 1","start":1,"end":4},{"label":"Grade 2","start":8,"end":16},{"label":"Grade 3","start":24,"end":36}]},{"tissue":"Ligament","color":"#e3a844","ranges":[{"label":"Grade 1","start":2,"end":8},{"label":"Grade 2","start":8,"end":24},{"label":"Grade 3","start":24,"end":48},{"label":"Graft (ACL)","start":48,"end":104}]},{"tissue":"Tendon","color":"#45b787","ranges":[{"label":"Acute","start":2,"end":6},{"label":"Chronic","start":12,"end":36},{"label":"Tear/Repair","start":16,"end":48}]},{"tissue":"Bone/Fracture","color":"#51c9d6","ranges":[{"label":"Typical","start":5,"end":12}]},{"tissue":"Skin","color":"#7dd3c0","ranges":[{"label":"Surface","start":1,"end":2},{"label":"Deep","start":4,"end":24}]},{"tissue":"Articular Cartilage","color":"#a361ea","ranges":[{"label":"Repair","start":36,"end":104},{"label":"May not heal","start":104,"end":110}]},{"tissue":"Peripheral Nerve","color":"#b88be0","ranges":[{"label":"Minor","start":16,"end":20},{"label":"Major","start":24,"end":48}]},{"tissue":"Disc","color":"#d94294","ranges":[{"label":"Herniation","start":12,"end":24}]},{"tissue":"Meniscus/Labrum","color":"#f7e06a","ranges":[{"label":"Repair","start":24,"end":48}]},{"tissue":"Brain Injury","color":"#8bc34a","ranges":[{"label":"Mild TBI","start":12,"end":16},{"label":"Severe (stroke/CNS)","start":24,"end":96}]}]},"biomechanicsTopicsData":{"BIOMECHANICS_TOPICS":[{"id":"core","n":1,"title":"Core Concepts","subtitle":"Master these foundations to understand every movement","tagline":"Anatomy + physics + engineering","accent":"#f472b6","examTip":"Understand the concepts, apply them in practice, and they carry you through any exam.","panels":[{"heading":"Biomechanics","kind":"list","items":["The study of the mechanical laws relating to the structure and function of living organisms","Combines anatomy, physics and engineering principles"]},{"heading":"Kinematics vs Kinetics","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"Kinematics","points":["Describes motion","Displacement, velocity, acceleration","Focuses on HOW the body moves"]},"right":{"title":"Kinetics","points":["Describes forces","Causes of motion","Focuses on WHY the body moves"]}},{"heading":"Motion","kind":"list","items":["A change in position of a body, or of a part of a body, with respect to time","Types: linear, angular, general (linear + angular)"]},{"heading":"Force","kind":"list","items":["A push or pull that can change the position, motion or shape of a body","External forces: gravity, ground reaction, muscle pull","Internal forces: muscle contraction"]},{"heading":"Torque (moment)","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Definition","value":"The turning effect of a force about an axis or a point"},{"label":"Formula","value":"Torque (τ) = Force (F) × Moment arm (d)"},{"label":"Unit","value":"Newton-metre (N·m)"}]},{"heading":"Equilibrium","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Definition","value":"Net force and net torque acting on a body are zero — no change in motion"},{"label":"Condition 1","value":"ΣF = 0 (net force = 0)"},{"label":"Condition 2","value":"Στ = 0 (net torque = 0)"}]},{"heading":"Centre of gravity (COG)","kind":"list","items":["The point in the body where the entire weight is considered to act","Varies with posture","Lower COG → more stability","Higher COG → less stability"]},{"heading":"Line of gravity (LOG)","kind":"list","items":["An imaginary vertical line drawn from the COG towards the centre of the earth","LOG inside the base of support → stable","LOG outside the base of support → unstable"]}]},{"id":"planes","n":2,"title":"Planes & Axes","subtitle":"Movement occurs in a plane around an axis","tagline":"Plane = surface · Axis = line","accent":"#a78bfa","examTip":"Sagittal → side-view movements. Frontal → front-view movements. Transverse → top-view movements.","panels":[{"heading":"Sagittal plane","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Divides the body","value":"Right & left parts"},{"label":"Axis","value":"Mediolateral (side to side)"},{"label":"Movements","value":"Flexion, extension"},{"label":"Examples","value":"Bending forward, standing up, walking (knee flexion)"}]},{"heading":"Frontal (coronal) plane","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Divides the body","value":"Anterior & posterior parts"},{"label":"Axis","value":"Anteroposterior (front to back)"},{"label":"Movements","value":"Abduction, adduction"},{"label":"Examples","value":"Lifting arms sideways, bringing the arm towards the body"}]},{"heading":"Transverse (horizontal) plane","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Divides the body","value":"Superior & inferior parts"},{"label":"Axis","value":"Vertical (up & down)"},{"label":"Movements","value":"Rotation"},{"label":"Examples","value":"Turning the head, rotating the trunk"}]},{"heading":"Quick recall","kind":"list","items":["Sagittal → side-view movements (flexion / extension)","Frontal → front-view movements (abduction / adduction)","Transverse → top-view movements (rotation)","Remember: a plane is a surface, an axis is a line"]}]},{"id":"newton","n":3,"title":"Newton's Laws of Motion","subtitle":"These laws explain how forces affect movement","tagline":"Inertia · F = ma · Action & reaction","accent":"#60a5fa","examTip":"These three laws are the foundation of all human movement and sport performance — analyse, then apply.","panels":[{"heading":"1 · Law of inertia","kind":"list","items":["A body at rest stays at rest; a body in motion stays in motion at the same speed and direction unless acted on by an unbalanced external force","At rest: a book stays on the table","In motion: a ball keeps rolling at the same speed","Clinical: when a bus stops suddenly, the body continues forward"]},{"heading":"1 · Key point","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Inertia","value":"The resistance of any object to a change in its state of rest or uniform motion"}]},{"heading":"2 · Law of acceleration","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Statement","value":"Acceleration is directly proportional to net force and inversely proportional to mass"},{"label":"Formula","value":"F = m × a  (Newton = 1 kg·m/s²)"},{"label":"Example","value":"Pushing an empty cart is easy; a loaded cart needs more force"},{"label":"Clinical","value":"In gait, greater muscle force produces faster acceleration of body segments"}]},{"heading":"3 · Law of action & reaction","kind":"list","items":["For every action there is an equal and opposite reaction","Jumping: you push the ground down (action), the ground pushes you up (reaction)","Action and reaction are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction"]},{"heading":"Quick summary","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Law of inertia","value":"Objects resist change in motion"},{"label":"Law of acceleration","value":"F = ma"},{"label":"Action & reaction","value":"Every action has an equal, opposite reaction"}]}]},{"id":"force-torque","n":4,"title":"Force & Torque","subtitle":"Force moves. Torque turns.","tagline":"F = m × a · τ = F × d","accent":"#fb923c","examTip":"Force = push or pull. Torque = turning effect. Movement = balance of forces.","panels":[{"heading":"Force","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Definition","value":"A push or pull that can change the state of rest or motion, or the shape of a body"},{"label":"Formula","value":"F = m × a  ·  Unit: Newton (N)"},{"label":"Types","value":"External (gravity, ground reaction, muscle pull) · Internal (muscle contraction)"}]},{"heading":"Torque (moment)","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Definition","value":"The turning effect of a force about an axis or point"},{"label":"Formula","value":"τ = F × d  ·  Unit: Newton-metre (N·m)"},{"label":"Moment arm (d)","value":"Perpendicular distance from the axis to the line of action of the force"},{"label":"Key point","value":"More force or more moment arm → more torque"}]},{"heading":"Internal vs external forces","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"Internal forces","points":["Produced within the body","Muscle contraction, joint reaction force, ligament tension, tendon pull","Help produce movement","Example: biceps pulling to flex the elbow"]},"right":{"title":"External forces","points":["Act on the body from outside","Gravity, ground reaction, friction, external resistance","May assist or oppose movement","Example: gravity pulling the body downward"]}},{"heading":"Mechanical advantage (MA)","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Formula","value":"MA = R / F (resistance ÷ applied force)"},{"label":"MA > 1","value":"Force advantage"},{"label":"MA < 1","value":"Speed advantage"},{"label":"MA = 1","value":"Neither"}]},{"heading":"Rapid summary","kind":"list","items":["Force causes linear acceleration → F = ma","Torque causes rotational motion → τ = F × d","Moment arm is the perpendicular distance","External forces come from outside the body","Mechanical advantage shows efficiency"]}]},{"id":"levers","n":5,"title":"Levers in the Human Body","subtitle":"Simple machines. Powerful movements.","tagline":"F = fulcrum · E = effort · R = resistance","accent":"#4ade80","examTip":"Levers help the body move more efficiently with less effort — know the order of each class.","panels":[{"heading":"What is a lever?","kind":"list","items":["A rigid bar that turns about a fixed point (fulcrum) to move a load (resistance) using effort (force)","In the body: bone = bar, joint = fulcrum, muscle = effort, body weight or load = resistance"]},{"heading":"1st class lever","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Arrangement","value":"Fulcrum between force and resistance (F – Fulcrum – R)"},{"label":"Example","value":"Nodding the head (atlanto-occipital joint)"}]},{"heading":"2nd class lever","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Arrangement","value":"Resistance between fulcrum and force (Fulcrum – R – F)"},{"label":"Example","value":"Rising onto the toes (plantarflexion at the ankle)"}]},{"heading":"3rd class lever","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Arrangement","value":"Force between fulcrum and resistance (Fulcrum – F – R)"},{"label":"Example","value":"Bending the elbow (biceps brachii action) — most common in the body"}]},{"heading":"Why levers matter","kind":"list","items":["They increase force, speed or range of motion","Different levers suit different tasks","The body uses levers for efficient and precise movement"]}]},{"id":"stability","n":6,"title":"Centre of Gravity & Stability","subtitle":"Stability is the ability to maintain balance and resist falling","tagline":"Wider base · Lower COG · More stable","accent":"#22d3ee","examTip":"Stability is the balance between the body and the environment — base, centre, mass and line of gravity.","panels":[{"heading":"Base of support (BOS)","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"Wide BOS","points":["More area to resist tilting forces","More stable"]},"right":{"title":"Narrow BOS","points":["Less area to resist tilting","Less stable"]}},{"heading":"Centre of gravity (COG)","kind":"list","items":["The point where the whole mass of the body is considered to be concentrated","In humans it sits at about the level of the 2nd sacral vertebra (S2)","Changes with posture and load"]},{"heading":"Line of gravity (LOG)","kind":"list","items":["An imaginary vertical line drawn downward from the COG","If the LOG falls within the base of support, you remain balanced","If it falls outside the BOS, you lose balance and fall"]},{"heading":"Stability increases with","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Wider base of support","value":"More area to resist tilting forces"},{"label":"Lower centre of gravity","value":"Less tendency to tip (e.g. a deep squat stance)"},{"label":"More body mass","value":"Greater resistance to external forces"},{"label":"LOG within the BOS","value":"Must fall inside the base to maintain balance"}]},{"heading":"Remember","kind":"list","items":["Wider BOS → more stability","Lower COG → more stability","More body mass → more stability","Line of gravity within BOS → stable"]}]},{"id":"contractions","n":7,"title":"Muscle Contractions","subtitle":"Muscles don't just move the body, they control how it moves","tagline":"Isometric · Concentric · Eccentric","accent":"#f87171","examTip":"Isometric = hold. Concentric = lift. Eccentric = lower. Agonist = do. Antagonist = oppose.","panels":[{"heading":"Isometric contraction","kind":"list","items":["Muscle contracts but does not change length — no movement occurs","Example: pushing against an immovable wall","Tension is produced, but the joint angle stays the same"]},{"heading":"Concentric contraction","kind":"list","items":["Muscle shortens while producing movement","Example: biceps shortening to lift a dumbbell (elbow flexion)","Muscle shortens, tension produces movement"]},{"heading":"Eccentric contraction","kind":"list","items":["Muscle lengthens while controlling movement","Example: biceps lengthening while lowering a dumbbell","Muscle lengthens, controls movement, opposes force"]},{"heading":"Roles of muscles in movement","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Agonist (prime mover)","value":"Main muscle responsible for producing a movement — e.g. biceps in elbow flexion"},{"label":"Antagonist","value":"Works opposite the agonist to control and slow the movement — e.g. triceps in elbow flexion"},{"label":"Synergist","value":"Assists the agonist, adding force and reducing its workload — e.g. brachialis"},{"label":"Fixator (stabiliser)","value":"Stabilises the origin so movement can occur smoothly — e.g. scapular stabilisers"}]},{"heading":"Key in one line","kind":"list","items":["Isometric → hold","Concentric → lift","Eccentric → lower","Agonist → do","Antagonist → oppose","Synergist → help","Fixator → hold steady"]}]},{"id":"length-tension","n":8,"title":"Muscle Length–Tension","subtitle":"The length of a muscle determines how much force it can produce","tagline":"Best overlap = best power","accent":"#facc15","examTip":"Too short = crowded. Too long = no overlap. Optimal = best overlap = best power.","panels":[{"heading":"The length–tension curve","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Active tension","value":"Force from actin–myosin cross-bridge formation"},{"label":"Passive tension","value":"Force from stretch of elastic elements (titin, tendons, connective tissue)"},{"label":"Total tension","value":"Active + passive; peaks at a length slightly longer than optimal"}]},{"heading":"Active tension","kind":"list","items":["Produced by actin–myosin cross-bridge formation","Increases from short length to optimal length","Decreases when the muscle is overstretched","More cross-bridges = more force"]},{"heading":"Passive tension","kind":"list","items":["Produced by elastic components (titin, connective tissue, tendon)","Minimal at short and mid muscle lengths","Increases as the muscle is stretched beyond optimal length"]},{"heading":"Sarcomere length & tension","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Too short","value":"Z-lines close, actin filaments overlap excessively → active tension ↓"},{"label":"Optimal","value":"Ideal overlap of actin & myosin → maximum active tension"},{"label":"Too long","value":"Actin–myosin overlap reduced, fewer cross-bridges → active tension ↓"}]},{"heading":"Why optimal length matters","kind":"list","items":["Maximum force production → more cross-bridges = more force","Better performance — muscles work most efficiently near optimal length","Injury prevention — too short or too long increases stress on muscle & connective tissue","Functional movement — during daily activities muscles operate near their optimal length for best results"]},{"heading":"Quick summary","kind":"list","items":["Active tension depends on cross-bridge formation","Passive tension depends on elastic stretch","Total tension is highest at or slightly beyond optimal length","Optimal length = best force, efficiency and function"]}]},{"id":"kinematic-quantities","n":9,"title":"Kinematic Quantities","subtitle":"The language used to describe any movement","tagline":"Distance · Velocity · Acceleration","accent":"#38bdf8","examTip":"Scalars have size only. Vectors have size and direction. Displacement, velocity and acceleration are vectors.","panels":[{"heading":"Scalar vs vector","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"Scalar","points":["Magnitude only","Distance, speed, mass, time","Example: walked 400 m"]},"right":{"title":"Vector","points":["Magnitude + direction","Displacement, velocity, acceleration, force","Example: 400 m north"]}},{"heading":"Distance vs displacement","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Distance","value":"Total path travelled, regardless of direction (scalar, metres)"},{"label":"Displacement","value":"Straight-line change in position from start to finish (vector, metres)"}]},{"heading":"Speed vs velocity","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Speed","value":"Distance ÷ time (m/s, scalar)"},{"label":"Velocity","value":"Displacement ÷ time (m/s, vector)"},{"label":"Clinical use","value":"Gait speed is a key marker of function and recovery"}]},{"heading":"Acceleration","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Definition","value":"Rate of change of velocity"},{"label":"Formula","value":"a = (final velocity − initial velocity) ÷ time  ·  Unit: m/s²"},{"label":"Deceleration","value":"Negative acceleration — slowing down, where most non-contact injuries occur"}]},{"heading":"Angular equivalents","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Angular displacement","value":"Change in joint angle (degrees or radians)"},{"label":"Angular velocity","value":"Change in joint angle ÷ time (°/s)"},{"label":"Angular acceleration","value":"Change in angular velocity ÷ time (°/s²)"}]},{"heading":"Quick summary","kind":"list","items":["Kinematics describes motion without asking what caused it","Linear quantities describe the whole body or a segment moving through space","Angular quantities describe rotation at joints","Every joint movement can be measured in both"]}]},{"id":"joint-kinematics","n":10,"title":"Joint Kinematics","subtitle":"How bones move, and how joint surfaces move within them","tagline":"Osteokinematics vs arthrokinematics","accent":"#c084fc","examTip":"Osteokinematics = what you see. Arthrokinematics = what happens inside the joint. Both must be normal for full range.","panels":[{"heading":"Osteokinematics","kind":"list","items":["Gross movement of bones through space, visible to the eye","Described in planes and axes (flexion, abduction, rotation)","Measured with a goniometer as range of motion"]},{"heading":"Arthrokinematics","kind":"list","items":["Small accessory movements of joint surfaces on one another","Roll, glide (slide) and spin","Not under voluntary control — restored with mobilisation, not stretching"]},{"heading":"Convex–concave rule","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Convex on concave","value":"Roll and glide occur in OPPOSITE directions (e.g. glenohumeral joint)"},{"label":"Concave on convex","value":"Roll and glide occur in the SAME direction (e.g. knee in open chain)"},{"label":"Why it matters","value":"Determines the direction of joint mobilisation used in treatment"}]},{"heading":"Degrees of freedom","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"1 degree","value":"Movement in one plane — hinge joints (elbow, interphalangeal)"},{"label":"2 degrees","value":"Movement in two planes — condyloid / saddle joints (wrist, thumb)"},{"label":"3 degrees","value":"Movement in three planes — ball-and-socket (shoulder, hip)"}]},{"heading":"Joint positions","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Open-packed (loose)","value":"Ligaments and capsule relaxed, joint surfaces least congruent — best position for mobilisation"},{"label":"Close-packed","value":"Maximum congruency and tension, most stable — least suited to mobilisation"}]},{"heading":"Quick summary","kind":"list","items":["Osteokinematics = bone movement you can see and measure","Arthrokinematics = roll, glide and spin inside the joint","Convex–concave rule guides mobilisation direction","Degrees of freedom set how many planes a joint can work in"]}]},{"id":"friction","n":11,"title":"Friction & Ground Reaction Force","subtitle":"Nothing moves without something to push against","tagline":"Grip · Traction · Reaction","accent":"#fbbf24","examTip":"No friction, no propulsion. Ground reaction force is Newton's third law in action at every step.","panels":[{"heading":"Friction","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Definition","value":"Force that opposes sliding between two surfaces in contact"},{"label":"Formula","value":"F = μ × N (coefficient of friction × normal force)"},{"label":"Static friction","value":"Resists the start of motion — always higher than kinetic"},{"label":"Kinetic friction","value":"Resists motion already in progress"}]},{"heading":"Friction in practice","kind":"list","items":["Too little friction → slipping, falls, loss of push-off","Too much friction → shoe grabs the surface, high rotational load on knee and ankle","Court surface, shoe outsole and footwork all change the coefficient"]},{"heading":"Ground reaction force (GRF)","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Definition","value":"The force the ground exerts back on the body during contact"},{"label":"Vertical component","value":"Supports body weight and drives jumping — 2–3× body weight in running"},{"label":"Horizontal component","value":"Braking and propulsion — friction makes it possible"},{"label":"Medio-lateral component","value":"Side-to-side control during cutting and lateral movement"}]},{"heading":"Loading & injury risk","kind":"list","items":["Rate of loading matters as much as peak force","Rapid, high GRF with poor absorption → bone and tendon overload","Eccentric strength and landing mechanics reduce peak loading rate"]},{"heading":"Quick summary","kind":"list","items":["Friction allows push-off, braking and change of direction","F = μ × N","GRF is equal and opposite to the force you apply to the ground","Manage magnitude AND rate of loading"]}]},{"id":"tissue-mechanics","n":12,"title":"Tissue Mechanics","subtitle":"How living tissue responds to load over time","tagline":"Stress · Strain · Viscoelasticity","accent":"#34d399","examTip":"Stress is the load inside the tissue, strain is the deformation. Tissue behaviour depends on how fast and how long you load it.","panels":[{"heading":"Stress & strain","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Stress","value":"Force per unit area inside a tissue (N/m² or Pascals)"},{"label":"Strain","value":"Deformation relative to original length (%, no unit)"},{"label":"Stiffness","value":"Stress ÷ strain — how much load a tissue takes per unit of deformation"}]},{"heading":"Stress–strain curve","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Toe region","value":"Crimped collagen fibres straighten — very little load needed"},{"label":"Elastic region","value":"Tissue returns to original length when load is removed"},{"label":"Plastic region","value":"Permanent deformation — micro-failure begins"},{"label":"Failure point","value":"Rupture of the tissue (sprain grade III, tendon tear)"}]},{"heading":"Viscoelastic properties","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Creep","value":"Gradual lengthening under a constant sustained load — basis of prolonged stretching and splinting"},{"label":"Stress relaxation","value":"Force falls over time when length is held constant"},{"label":"Rate dependency","value":"Tissue is stiffer and more brittle when loaded fast, more compliant when loaded slowly"},{"label":"Hysteresis","value":"Energy lost as heat during a load–unload cycle"}]},{"heading":"Load types on tissue","kind":"list","items":["Tension — pulling apart (tendon, ligament, muscle)","Compression — pressing together (cartilage, bone, disc)","Shear — sliding across (disc, fascia interfaces)","Torsion — twisting (long bones, tibia)","Bending — combined tension and compression"]},{"heading":"Clinical relevance","kind":"list","items":["Warm-up reduces stiffness and hysteresis, improving tolerance","Slow, sustained loading uses creep to increase length","Repeated sub-failure loading without recovery causes fatigue failure — overuse injury","Graded progressive loading remodels tissue to be stiffer and stronger"]},{"heading":"Quick summary","kind":"list","items":["Stress = internal load, strain = deformation","Elastic = recoverable, plastic = permanent","Tissue behaviour changes with rate and duration of load","Progressive loading builds capacity; unmanaged repetition breaks it"]}]},{"id":"gait","n":13,"title":"The Gait Cycle","subtitle":"One full sequence from heel strike to the next heel strike on the same foot","tagline":"60% stance · 40% swing","accent":"#f97316","examTip":"Stance is about 60% of the cycle, swing 40%. Know the eight sub-phases and what each demands.","panels":[{"heading":"Terminology","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Gait cycle (stride)","value":"Heel strike on one foot to the next heel strike on the SAME foot"},{"label":"Step","value":"Heel strike on one foot to heel strike on the opposite foot"},{"label":"Cadence","value":"Number of steps per minute"},{"label":"Double support","value":"Both feet on the ground — present in walking, absent in running"}]},{"heading":"Stance phase (≈60%)","kind":"list","items":["Initial contact (heel strike) — begins loading","Loading response — shock absorption, knee flexes, foot pronates","Mid-stance — single-limb support, body passes over the foot","Terminal stance (heel off) — hip extends, ankle plantarflexes","Pre-swing (toe off) — propulsion and transfer of weight"]},{"heading":"Swing phase (≈40%)","kind":"list","items":["Initial swing — limb accelerates, hip and knee flex to clear the ground","Mid-swing — limb advances beneath the body","Terminal swing — knee extends, foot positioned for the next contact"]},{"heading":"Functional tasks","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Weight acceptance","value":"Initial contact + loading response"},{"label":"Single-limb support","value":"Mid-stance + terminal stance"},{"label":"Limb advancement","value":"Pre-swing through terminal swing"}]},{"heading":"Common deviations","kind":"list","items":["Antalgic gait — shortened stance on the painful limb","Trendelenburg — pelvic drop from hip abductor weakness","Foot drop / steppage — dorsiflexor weakness, high knee lift","Circumduction — limb swung outward to clear the ground"]},{"heading":"Quick summary","kind":"list","items":["Stance ≈ 60%, swing ≈ 40% of the cycle","Stance has five sub-phases, swing has three","Goals: stability, shock absorption, propulsion, energy conservation","Deviations point to specific weakness or pain"]}]},{"id":"posture","n":14,"title":"Posture & Alignment","subtitle":"The position from which every movement begins","tagline":"Line of gravity through the body","accent":"#5eead4","examTip":"Ideal posture minimises muscular effort and joint stress. Assess in all three views before treating.","panels":[{"heading":"Ideal alignment (side view)","kind":"list","items":["Slightly anterior to the lateral malleolus","Slightly anterior to the knee joint axis","Through or slightly posterior to the hip joint","Through the shoulder joint and the ear lobe"]},{"heading":"Static vs dynamic posture","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"Static posture","points":["Alignment at rest — standing, sitting, lying","Assessed visually or with a plumb line","Reflects habitual tissue length"]},"right":{"title":"Dynamic posture","points":["Alignment during movement","Assessed with squat, gait, single-leg tasks","Reflects control and strength"]}},{"heading":"Common faults","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Forward head posture","value":"Increases load on cervical extensors, common with desk and phone use"},{"label":"Rounded shoulders","value":"Tight pectorals, weak scapular retractors — reduces overhead capacity"},{"label":"Excessive lumbar lordosis","value":"Anterior pelvic tilt, tight hip flexors, weak abdominals & glutes"},{"label":"Flat back / posterior tilt","value":"Reduced lumbar curve, poor shock absorption"}]},{"heading":"Why posture matters mechanically","kind":"list","items":["Poor alignment lengthens moment arms and multiplies joint torque","Sustained mal-alignment causes creep and adaptive tissue shortening","Muscle at a non-optimal length produces less force (length–tension)","Efficient posture reduces energy cost of standing and moving"]},{"heading":"Quick summary","kind":"list","items":["Good posture keeps the line of gravity near joint axes","Static posture shows habit, dynamic posture shows control","Faults change torque, muscle length and energy cost","Correct with mobility + strength + habit, not cueing alone"]}]},{"id":"work-power","n":15,"title":"Work, Power & Energy","subtitle":"How much you do, and how fast you do it","tagline":"Work × rate = power","accent":"#e879f9","examTip":"Work is force × distance. Power is work ÷ time. Athletic performance is almost always about power.","panels":[{"heading":"Work","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Definition","value":"Force applied over a distance in the direction of the force"},{"label":"Formula","value":"W = F × d  ·  Unit: Joule (J)"},{"label":"Positive work","value":"Concentric — muscle shortens and produces movement"},{"label":"Negative work","value":"Eccentric — muscle lengthens and absorbs energy"}]},{"heading":"Power","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Definition","value":"Rate of doing work"},{"label":"Formula","value":"P = W ÷ t = F × velocity  ·  Unit: Watt (W)"},{"label":"Key point","value":"Two athletes can do the same work; the more powerful one does it faster"}]},{"heading":"Energy","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Kinetic energy","value":"KE = ½ m v² — energy of motion, rises sharply with speed"},{"label":"Potential energy","value":"PE = m g h — energy of position or height"},{"label":"Strain energy","value":"Energy stored in stretched tendon and fascia, reused in the stretch-shortening cycle"},{"label":"Conservation","value":"Energy is transferred between forms, not created or destroyed"}]},{"heading":"Momentum & impulse","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Momentum","value":"p = m × v — quantity of motion carried by the body"},{"label":"Impulse","value":"Force × time — changes momentum"},{"label":"Application","value":"Landing over a longer time reduces peak force for the same impulse"}]},{"heading":"Quick summary","kind":"list","items":["Work = force × distance (Joules)","Power = work ÷ time (Watts) — the currency of sport","Elastic energy stored in tendon reduces metabolic cost","Lengthening the time of impact lowers peak landing force"]}]}]},"physiologyTopicsData":{"PHYSIOLOGY_TOPICS":[{"id":"energy-systems","n":1,"title":"Energy Systems & Bioenergetics","subtitle":"Every contraction is paid for in ATP — only the payment method changes","tagline":"ATP-PCr · Glycolytic · Oxidative","accent":"#f97316","examTip":"The systems never work alone — they overlap. Duration and intensity decide which one dominates.","panels":[{"heading":"ATP-PCr (phosphagen) system","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Dominates","value":"0–10 seconds of maximal effort"},{"label":"Fuel","value":"Stored ATP and creatine phosphate in the muscle"},{"label":"Oxygen","value":"Anaerobic — no oxygen required"},{"label":"Sport examples","value":"Serve, smash, jump, 10 m sprint, 1RM lift"},{"label":"Recovery","value":"≈50% PCr restored in 30 s, ≈95% in 3–5 min"}]},{"heading":"Glycolytic (lactic) system","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Dominates","value":"≈10 seconds to 2 minutes"},{"label":"Fuel","value":"Muscle glycogen and blood glucose"},{"label":"Yield","value":"2 ATP per glucose (anaerobic) — fast but limited"},{"label":"By-product","value":"Lactate + H⁺; the H⁺ accumulation, not lactate, drives acidosis"},{"label":"Sport examples","value":"Long padel rally, 400 m, repeated sprints"}]},{"heading":"Oxidative (aerobic) system","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Dominates","value":"Beyond ≈2 minutes, and all recovery between efforts"},{"label":"Fuel","value":"Carbohydrate, fat, and protein in extreme cases"},{"label":"Yield","value":"≈30–32 ATP per glucose · ≈100+ ATP per fatty acid"},{"label":"Site","value":"Mitochondria — Krebs cycle + electron transport chain"},{"label":"Sport examples","value":"Match-long work capacity, between-point recovery"}]},{"heading":"Interplay & crossover","kind":"list","items":["All three systems are always active — the mix shifts with intensity and duration","At low intensity fat supplies most energy; as intensity rises carbohydrate takes over (crossover concept)","Intermittent sports rely on PCr for the effort and the aerobic system to restore it","A bigger aerobic engine means faster PCr resynthesis, so repeat-sprint ability improves"]},{"heading":"Anaerobic vs aerobic training targets","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"Anaerobic focus","points":["Short maximal efforts with full recovery","Develops PCr and glycolytic capacity","Rest:work ratios of 1:5 to 1:20","Improves peak power and repeat-sprint tolerance"]},"right":{"title":"Aerobic focus","points":["Continuous or long-interval work","Raises VO₂max, mitochondrial density and capillarisation","Rest:work ratios of 1:1 or continuous","Improves recovery between high-intensity efforts"]}},{"heading":"Quick summary","kind":"list","items":["ATP-PCr → seconds, no oxygen, highest power","Glycolysis → seconds to minutes, fast but acidifying","Oxidative → minutes to hours, highest capacity, lowest power","Train the system your sport actually taxes"]}]},{"id":"vo2max","n":2,"title":"VO₂max & Aerobic Capacity","subtitle":"The ceiling on how much oxygen you can use per minute","tagline":"VO₂ = Q × (a-vO₂ difference)","accent":"#22d3ee","examTip":"A plateau in VO₂ despite rising workload is the defining criterion of a true VO₂max test.","panels":[{"heading":"Definition & units","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"VO₂max","value":"Maximum rate of oxygen uptake, utilisation and delivery during maximal exercise"},{"label":"Absolute","value":"L/min — favours larger athletes"},{"label":"Relative","value":"mL/kg/min — used for comparison and for weight-bearing sport"},{"label":"Fick equation","value":"VO₂ = cardiac output × arterio-venous oxygen difference"}]},{"heading":"Criteria for a true maximal test","kind":"list","items":["Plateau in VO₂ (< 150 mL/min rise) despite increasing workload","Heart rate within ~10 bpm of age-predicted max (220 − age, used with caution)","Respiratory exchange ratio ≥ 1.10","Blood lactate ≥ 8 mmol/L","RPE ≥ 17 on the 6–20 Borg scale"]},{"heading":"What limits VO₂max","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Central","value":"Maximal cardiac output — the main limiter in most athletes"},{"label":"Peripheral","value":"Capillary density, mitochondrial volume, oxidative enzymes"},{"label":"Pulmonary","value":"Rarely limiting except in elite endurance athletes"},{"label":"Blood","value":"Haemoglobin mass and plasma volume"}]},{"heading":"Field & lab tests","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Gold standard","value":"Graded exercise test with expired gas analysis"},{"label":"Yo-Yo IR1 / 30-15 IFT","value":"Intermittent field tests — best match for court and field sport"},{"label":"Cooper 12-min run","value":"VO₂max ≈ (distance in m − 504.9) ÷ 44.73"},{"label":"Multistage 20 m shuttle","value":"Widely used in team sport, needs level and shuttle tables"}]},{"heading":"Trainability","kind":"list","items":["Typical improvement 5–20% with 8–12 weeks of structured training","Genetics account for a large share of both baseline and response","High-intensity intervals (90–100% VO₂max, 3–5 min) are the most time-efficient stimulus","Detraining costs measurable capacity within 2–4 weeks of inactivity"]},{"heading":"Quick summary","kind":"list","items":["VO₂max = the size of the aerobic engine","Fick: delivery (Q) × extraction (a-vO₂ diff)","Plateau defines a true max test","Central factors usually limit it, peripheral factors refine it"]}]},{"id":"lactate-threshold","n":3,"title":"Lactate Threshold & Intensity Zones","subtitle":"Not how big the engine is — how much of it you can hold","tagline":"LT1 · LT2 / MLSS","accent":"#facc15","examTip":"VO₂max sets the ceiling; the lactate threshold decides what fraction of it you can sustain.","panels":[{"heading":"The two thresholds","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"LT1 (aerobic threshold)","value":"First rise above resting lactate, ≈2 mmol/L — top of easy, conversational work"},{"label":"LT2 / MLSS","value":"Highest intensity where lactate production and clearance still balance, ≈4 mmol/L"},{"label":"Critical power","value":"A power-based equivalent of the same sustainable boundary"},{"label":"Above LT2","value":"Lactate and H⁺ accumulate progressively — time to exhaustion becomes short"}]},{"heading":"Why lactate is not the villain","kind":"list","items":["Lactate is a fuel — shuttled to other fibres, the heart and the liver and oxidised","Acidosis comes from H⁺ accumulation, not from lactate itself","Blood lactate is a marker of the balance between production and clearance, not of 'failure'","Trained athletes clear lactate faster at the same absolute workload"]},{"heading":"Three-zone intensity model","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Zone 1 (below LT1)","value":"Easy aerobic — builds mitochondria and aids recovery"},{"label":"Zone 2 (LT1–LT2)","value":"Threshold work — raises sustainable pace/power"},{"label":"Zone 3 (above LT2)","value":"VO₂max and anaerobic work — small doses, high return, high cost"},{"label":"Polarised approach","value":"Roughly 80% zone 1, 20% zone 3 in many endurance programmes"}]},{"heading":"Assessment options","kind":"list","items":["Incremental test with capillary blood lactate every 3–4 min stage","Ventilatory thresholds from gas analysis (VT1, VT2) as non-invasive proxies","Talk test — LT1 sits near the point where full sentences become hard","30-min time trial average heart rate approximates LT2 in trained athletes"]},{"heading":"Quick summary","kind":"list","items":["LT1 = easy/moderate boundary, LT2 = sustainable ceiling","Threshold shifts with training even when VO₂max does not","Lactate is fuel and a signal, not waste","Zone discipline matters more than total volume"]}]},{"id":"cardiac-output","n":4,"title":"Cardiovascular Response to Exercise","subtitle":"How delivery scales up from rest to maximal effort","tagline":"Q = HR × SV","accent":"#f472b6","examTip":"Stroke volume plateaus around 40–60% VO₂max in untrained people; in athletes it keeps rising towards max.","panels":[{"heading":"Cardiac output","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Formula","value":"Q = heart rate × stroke volume"},{"label":"Rest","value":"≈5 L/min (70 bpm × 70 mL)"},{"label":"Trained max","value":"30–40 L/min in elite endurance athletes"},{"label":"Untrained max","value":"≈20–25 L/min"}]},{"heading":"Acute responses to exercise","kind":"list","items":["Heart rate rises linearly with workload up to maximum","Stroke volume rises then plateaus (later and higher in trained athletes)","Systolic blood pressure rises; diastolic stays flat or falls slightly","Blood is redistributed — up to 85% of flow to working muscle","a-vO₂ difference widens as muscle extracts more oxygen"]},{"heading":"Chronic adaptations","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"Endurance training","points":["Eccentric hypertrophy — larger ventricular chamber","Higher stroke volume, lower resting HR","Increased plasma volume and capillary density","Faster HR recovery after effort"]},"right":{"title":"Resistance training","points":["Concentric hypertrophy — thicker ventricular wall","Little change in chamber size","Transient large blood-pressure spikes during lifts","Modest change in resting HR"]}},{"heading":"Monitoring markers","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Resting heart rate","value":"Falls with aerobic fitness; a sustained rise suggests fatigue or illness"},{"label":"HR recovery","value":"Drop in the first 60 s after effort — faster is fitter"},{"label":"HRV (rMSSD)","value":"Parasympathetic marker — trend matters far more than any single day"},{"label":"Double product","value":"HR × systolic BP — an index of myocardial workload"}]},{"heading":"Quick summary","kind":"list","items":["Q = HR × SV, and VO₂ = Q × a-vO₂ difference","Training raises max Q mostly through stroke volume","Endurance and resistance training remodel the heart differently","Resting HR, HR recovery and HRV trends track readiness"]}]},{"id":"hr-zones","n":5,"title":"Exercise Prescription & FITT-VP","subtitle":"Turning physiology into a weekly plan","tagline":"Frequency · Intensity · Time · Type · Volume · Progression","accent":"#4ade80","examTip":"ACSM baseline: 150 min moderate or 75 min vigorous aerobic activity per week, plus 2+ resistance sessions.","panels":[{"heading":"FITT-VP","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Frequency","value":"Sessions per week — 3–5 aerobic, 2–3 per muscle group for resistance"},{"label":"Intensity","value":"%HRmax, %HRR, %VO₂max, RPE or velocity/power"},{"label":"Time","value":"Duration per session — 20–60 min aerobic depending on intensity"},{"label":"Type","value":"Mode — rhythmic aerobic, resistance, flexibility, neuromotor"},{"label":"Volume","value":"Total dose (frequency × intensity × time), often as MET-min/week"},{"label":"Progression","value":"Advance one variable at a time, ≈5–10% per week"}]},{"heading":"Intensity anchors","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Light","value":"57–63% HRmax · 30–39% HRR · RPE 9–11"},{"label":"Moderate","value":"64–76% HRmax · 40–59% HRR · RPE 12–13"},{"label":"Vigorous","value":"77–95% HRmax · 60–89% HRR · RPE 14–17"},{"label":"Karvonen (HRR)","value":"Target HR = ((HRmax − HRrest) × %) + HRrest"}]},{"heading":"Resistance prescription","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Strength","value":"≥85% 1RM · 2–6 reps · 2–5 sets · 2–5 min rest"},{"label":"Hypertrophy","value":"67–85% 1RM · 6–12 reps · 3–6 sets · 30–90 s rest"},{"label":"Power","value":"30–60% 1RM (upper) / 0–60% (lower) · 3–6 fast reps · full recovery"},{"label":"Endurance","value":"≤67% 1RM · 12+ reps · short rest"}]},{"heading":"Pre-participation screening","kind":"list","items":["ACSM screening algorithm: current activity level, known disease, signs/symptoms, desired intensity","Medical clearance is needed for symptomatic individuals or known cardiac, metabolic or renal disease","PAR-Q+ is a practical self-screen for apparently healthy adults","Absolute contraindications to testing include acute MI, unstable angina, uncontrolled arrhythmia and acute infection"]},{"heading":"Quick summary","kind":"list","items":["FITT-VP is the full prescription checklist","Use HRR/Karvonen for individualised targets","Load parameters differ for strength, hypertrophy, power and endurance","Screen first, then progress one variable at a time"]}]},{"id":"thermo-altitude","n":6,"title":"Environmental Physiology","subtitle":"Heat, cold, altitude and travel change the same session completely","tagline":"Heat · Hydration · Altitude","accent":"#38bdf8","examTip":"Exertional heat stroke is core temp > 40 °C with CNS dysfunction — cool first, transport second.","panels":[{"heading":"Thermoregulation in the heat","kind":"list","items":["Heat is lost by radiation, conduction, convection and evaporation — evaporation dominates in exercise","High humidity blunts evaporative loss, so core temperature rises faster","Cardiovascular drift: HR rises at fixed workload as plasma volume falls","Sweat rates of 0.5–2.5 L/h are common in hot conditions"]},{"heading":"Heat illness spectrum","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Heat cramps","value":"Painful muscle spasm — rest, fluids and sodium"},{"label":"Heat exhaustion","value":"Fatigue, dizziness, core temp < 40 °C, intact cognition"},{"label":"Exertional heat stroke","value":"Core temp > 40 °C + CNS dysfunction — medical emergency"},{"label":"Treatment priority","value":"Cold-water immersion, cool first then transport"}]},{"heading":"Hydration strategy","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Sweat rate","value":"(pre-weight − post-weight + fluid intake) ÷ hours"},{"label":"During","value":"Match fluid to sweat loss; keep losses under 2% body mass"},{"label":"After","value":"125–150% of the deficit with sodium to retain it"},{"label":"Hyponatraemia","value":"Over-drinking plain water — dangerous, not protective"}]},{"heading":"Altitude & acclimatisation","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Acute response","value":"Lower PO₂ → hyperventilation, higher HR, reduced VO₂max (≈1–2% per 100 m above 1500 m)"},{"label":"Acclimatisation","value":"Higher EPO and haemoglobin mass over 2–4 weeks"},{"label":"Live high, train low","value":"Sleep at altitude for adaptation, train low to keep intensity"},{"label":"Heat acclimatisation","value":"5–14 days: earlier sweating, more dilute sweat, expanded plasma volume"}]},{"heading":"Quick summary","kind":"list","items":["Humidity, not just temperature, drives heat risk","Individual sweat rate should set the drinking plan","Heat stroke is cooled on site before transport","Altitude cuts VO₂max acutely and rewards planned acclimatisation"]}]}]},"electrotherapyData":{"CONTRAINDICATIONS":{"absolute":["Cardiac pacemaker / implantable electronic devices","Malignancy over or near the treatment area","Thrombophlebitis / DVT","Active haemorrhage","Pregnancy — over abdomen, lumbar region & pelvis","Epilepsy (for some modalities, e.g. IFT, SWD, TENS over head/neck)","Unhealed fractures","Acute infection / open wounds","Over carotid sinus, eyes, mouth or heart","Sensory loss (risk of burns)"],"relative":["Severe cardiovascular disease","Uncontrolled hypertension","Diabetes mellitus with neuropathy","Metal implants / prosthesis (use with caution)","Varicose veins","Acute inflammation","Skin conditions — rashes, eczema, dermatitis","Oedema (in acute stage)","Elderly / very young patients"],"modalitySpecific":[{"modality":"TENS","avoid":"Carotid sinus, eyes, broken skin","caution":"Lower intensity in cardiac patients"},{"modality":"Ultrasound","avoid":"Epiphyseal plates in children","caution":"Avoid over malignancy, eyes, testes, uterus"},{"modality":"SWD","avoid":"All metal objects; pregnancy, pacemaker patients","caution":"Do not cross the electrodes"},{"modality":"IFT","avoid":"Over heart & carotid sinus","caution":"Lower intensity in sensitive patients"},{"modality":"Cryotherapy","avoid":"Cold intolerance, impaired sensation","caution":"Check skin sensation before & during"},{"modality":"Heat therapy","avoid":"Acute inflammation, impaired sensation","caution":"Do not use over areas of malignancy"},{"modality":"NMES","avoid":"Denervated muscles","caution":"Avoid over heart, head or neck"},{"modality":"FES","avoid":"Spasticity & seizures","caution":"Check for fatigue & skin irritation"}],"generalRules":["Explain the procedure & get consent","Check the equipment before use","Inspect the skin before & after treatment","Use the correct dosage & parameters","Increase intensity gradually","Never leave the patient unattended","Monitor patient response throughout"],"takeaways":["Right patient, right modality, right parameters","Contraindications first, then treatment","Screen → assess → treat → reassess","Safety = effective therapy","Always THINK before you STIMULATE"]},"ELECTROTHERAPY_MODALITIES":[{"id":"tens","n":1,"title":"TENS","subtitle":"Transcutaneous Electrical Nerve Stimulation","tagline":"Main use · Pain relief","accent":"#22d3ee","examTip":"Conventional TENS → gate control. Acupuncture-like TENS → endorphin release.","panels":[{"heading":"Types","kind":"list","items":["Conventional TENS (high frequency)","Acupuncture-like TENS (low frequency)","Burst TENS (hybrid)"]},{"heading":"Indications","kind":"list","items":["Acute & chronic pain","Post-operative pain","Musculoskeletal pain","Neuropathic pain","Labour pain","Osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis"]},{"heading":"Parameters","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Frequency","value":"Conventional 50–100 Hz · Acupuncture-like 1–4 Hz · Burst 2–50 Hz"},{"label":"Pulse duration","value":"50–150 µs"},{"label":"Intensity","value":"Strong but comfortable, no muscle contraction"},{"label":"Mode","value":"Continuous"}]},{"heading":"Main effects","kind":"list","items":["Pain relief (gate control theory)","Endorphin release","Muscle spasm ↓","Local blood flow ↑","Relaxation"]},{"heading":"Contraindications","kind":"list","tone":"warn","items":["Pacemaker","Malignancy","Pregnancy (abdomen & lumbosacral)","Areas of impaired sensation","Over carotid sinus","Skin infection / open wounds","Epilepsy (relative)"]},{"heading":"Avoid placing over","kind":"list","tone":"warn","items":["Head","Eyes","Heart region","Broken / fractured skin"]}]},{"id":"ultrasound","n":2,"title":"Therapeutic Ultrasound","subtitle":"Deep heating · Non-invasive · Effective","tagline":"1 MHz = deeper · 3 MHz = superficial","accent":"#a78bfa","examTip":"Frequency decides DEPTH, mode decides HEAT, intensity decides EFFECT.","panels":[{"heading":"Frequency","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"1 MHz","points":["Penetration 3–5 cm","Deep tissues","Chronic conditions, large joints, muscles, ligaments"]},"right":{"title":"3 MHz","points":["Penetration 1–2 cm","Superficial tissues","Acute conditions, tendons, small joints, superficial lesions"]}},{"heading":"Mode","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"Continuous","points":["100% duty cycle","Produces more heat","Used in chronic conditions"]},"right":{"title":"Pulsed","points":["Duty cycle 20–50%","Less heat, more mechanical effect","Used in acute conditions"]}},{"heading":"Intensity","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Measured in","value":"W/cm²"},{"label":"Usual range","value":"0.1–2.0 W/cm²"},{"label":"Rule","value":"More intensity → more thermal effect. Start low, build up."}]},{"heading":"Thermal effects","kind":"list","items":["Tissue temperature ↑","Blood flow ↑","Metabolic rate ↑","Pain relief","Tissue extensibility ↑","Speeds up healing"]},{"heading":"Non-thermal effects","kind":"list","items":["Micro-massage","Cavitation","Acoustic streaming","Cell membrane permeability ↑","Inflammation ↓"]},{"heading":"Indications","kind":"list","items":["Chronic soft tissue injuries","Tendinitis, bursitis","Osteoarthritis","Myofascial pain","Muscle strains (healing phase)","Ligament sprains (healing phase)","Scar tissue & adhesions"]},{"heading":"Contraindications","kind":"list","tone":"warn","items":["Malignancy","Over heart, eyes, brain, testes","Pregnancy (abdomen & lumbosacral)","Active TB","Over growth plates in children","Pacemaker","Bleeding disorders","Acute inflammation / infection","Loss of sensation","Carotid sinus, thyroid, spine (direct)"]}]},{"id":"swd","n":3,"title":"Shortwave Diathermy (SWD)","subtitle":"Deep heating · Pain relief · Tissue healing","tagline":"Frequency 27.12 MHz","accent":"#f472b6","examTip":"27.12 MHz, continuous = more heat, pulsed = less heat. Deep heating → better healing.","panels":[{"heading":"Frequency & wavelength","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Frequency","value":"27.12 MHz"},{"label":"Wavelength","value":"11 metres"}]},{"heading":"Mode","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"Continuous","points":["100% duty cycle","Produces more heat","Used for chronic conditions"]},"right":{"title":"Pulsed","points":["Duty cycle 5–50%","Less heat, more comfort","Used for acute conditions"]}},{"heading":"Thermal effects","kind":"list","items":["Tissue temperature ↑ (deep heat)","Blood circulation ↑","Metabolic rate ↑","Collagen extensibility ↑","Pain ↓","Muscle spasm ↓"]},{"heading":"Indications","kind":"list","items":["Chronic arthritis","Bursitis & tendinitis","Muscle spasm","Joint stiffness","Chronic low back pain","Soft tissue injuries (subacute/chronic)","Pre-stretch / pre-exercise"]},{"heading":"Types of application","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"Capacitive (C)","points":["Plates placed parallel","Effect on superficial tissues","More comfortable"]},"right":{"title":"Inductive (I)","points":["Plates placed at right angles","Deeper penetration","More intense heating"]}},{"heading":"Important precautions","kind":"list","tone":"warn","items":["Remove all metal objects","Keep applicators dry, use automatic movement","Patient should not feel excessive heat","Adequate spacing between applicators","Hydrate the patient"]},{"heading":"Contraindications","kind":"list","tone":"warn","items":["Pacemaker","Malignancy","Pregnancy (abdomen, eyes, testes)","Active TB","Over heart, eyes, testes","Metal implants in treatment area","Acute inflammation","Loss of sensation"]}]},{"id":"ift","n":4,"title":"Interferential Therapy (IFT)","subtitle":"Pain relief · Deep penetration · Muscle relaxation","tagline":"Carrier 4,000 Hz · Beat 0–150 Hz","accent":"#fb923c","examTip":"IFT = two medium-frequency currents interfere → create a low-frequency current deep in tissue.","panels":[{"heading":"What is IFT?","kind":"list","items":["Two medium frequency currents intersect in the body","They produce a low frequency 'beat' current","The beat frequency produces the therapeutic effect"]},{"heading":"Parameters","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Carrier frequency","value":"4,000 Hz"},{"label":"Beat frequency","value":"0–150 Hz"},{"label":"Amplitude","value":"As tolerated"},{"label":"Sweep","value":"1–10 Hz (for comfort)"},{"label":"Treatment time","value":"10–20 min"},{"label":"Intensity","value":"Strong but comfortable"}]},{"heading":"Electrode placement","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"2-pole (convergent)","points":["Electrodes placed nearby","Current crosses in the tissue","For smaller areas"]},"right":{"title":"4-pole (quadripolar)","points":["Electrodes placed far apart","Current crosses in the middle","For larger / deeper areas"]}},{"heading":"Physiological effects","kind":"list","items":["Pain relief (gate control theory)","Muscle relaxation","Local blood flow ↑","Muscle spasm ↓","Tissue extensibility ↑","Oedema ↓"]},{"heading":"Indications","kind":"list","items":["Acute & chronic pain","Muscle spasm","Osteoarthritis","Post-operative pain","Soft tissue injuries","Oedema","Neuropathic pain"]},{"heading":"Contraindications","kind":"list","tone":"warn","items":["Pacemaker","Malignancy","Pregnancy (abdomen & lumbosacral)","Over heart, eyes, testes","Metal implants in treatment area","Acute inflammation / infection","Loss of sensation"]}]},{"id":"cryotherapy","n":5,"title":"Cryotherapy","subtitle":"Cold therapy = pain relief + inflammation control","tagline":"Cold for ACUTE, heat for CHRONIC","accent":"#60a5fa","examTip":"Use cold in the ACUTE phase, heat in the CHRONIC phase.","panels":[{"heading":"Physiological effects","kind":"list","items":["Vasoconstriction → blood flow ↓, swelling & inflammation ↓","Muscle spasm ↓, muscle guarding ↓","Pain threshold ↑ (gate control theory)","Metabolic rate ↓, oxygen demand ↓","Nerve conduction ↓ (sensory & motor)","Delayed onset muscle soreness ↓"]},{"heading":"Methods","kind":"list","items":["Ice pack","Ice massage","Cold compress","Cold immersion","Cryo-spray","Ice cup massage"]},{"heading":"Indications","kind":"list","items":["Acute pain & inflammation","Muscle strain / sprain","Acute sports injuries","Post-operative pain & swelling","Headache / migraine","Spasticity control","Minor burns"]},{"heading":"Contraindications","kind":"list","tone":"warn","items":["Raynaud's disease","Cold hypersensitivity","Open wounds / infections","Severe peripheral vascular disease","Impaired sensation / neuropathy","Cryoglobulinemia","Over carotid sinus"]},{"heading":"Key points to remember","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Duration","value":"10–20 min per session"},{"label":"Temperature","value":"Ideally 0°C – 15°C"},{"label":"Skin","value":"Always protect it — use a towel or cloth barrier"}]}]},{"id":"heat","n":6,"title":"Heat Therapy","subtitle":"Heat = relaxation + pain relief + tissue healing","tagline":"Superficial 2–3 cm · Deep > 3 cm","accent":"#f87171","examTip":"Heat is for CHRONIC, not ACUTE. Moist heat penetrates better than dry heat.","panels":[{"heading":"Superficial vs deep heating","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"Superficial heating","points":["Acts on skin & superficial tissues (2–3 cm)","Blood flow ↑","Pain relief","Muscle relaxation"]},"right":{"title":"Deep heating","points":["Penetrates deeper tissues (> 3 cm)","Circulation ↑","Relaxes deep muscles","Promotes healing"]}},{"heading":"Methods","kind":"list","items":["Hot packs","Moist hot pack","Paraffin wax bath","Hydrocollator pack","Whirlpool bath","Infrared radiation","Shortwave diathermy"]},{"heading":"Physiological effects","kind":"list","items":["Blood flow ↑ (vasodilation)","Tissue temperature ↑","Metabolic rate ↑","Collagen extensibility ↑","Pain ↓ (gate control theory)","Muscle spasm ↓","Nerve conduction ↑","Promotes tissue healing"]},{"heading":"Indications","kind":"list","items":["Chronic musculoskeletal pain","Muscle spasm","Joint stiffness","Myofascial tightness","Chronic arthritis","Before exercise (warm-up)","Subacute & chronic conditions"]},{"heading":"Contraindications","kind":"list","tone":"warn","items":["Acute inflammation / acute injury","Malignancy","Areas of impaired sensation","Severe cardiovascular disease","Active bleeding","Pregnancy (abdomen & lumbosacral)","Open wounds / skin infections"]},{"heading":"Key points to remember","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Temperature","value":"Should feel hot, not burning — check it"},{"label":"Duration","value":"10–20 min per session"},{"label":"Moist heat","value":"Penetrates better than dry heat"},{"label":"Skin","value":"Protect with 6–8 layers of towel or a barrier"}]}]},{"id":"nmes","n":7,"title":"NMES / FES","subtitle":"Neuromuscular electrical stimulation","tagline":"Electrical current · Muscle contraction · Functional recovery","accent":"#4ade80","examTip":"NMES = strengthening. FES = producing movement to improve function.","panels":[{"heading":"NMES vs FES","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"NMES","points":["Strengthening & re-education","Produces muscle contraction only","No functional movement","Muscle targeted directly"]},"right":{"title":"FES","points":["Restores functional activities","Produces functional movement","Helps gait, grasp, dorsi/plantar flexion","Nerve stimulated to produce movement"]}},{"heading":"Muscle stimulation","kind":"list","items":["Place electrode over the motor point","Gives maximum contraction with minimum intensity"]},{"heading":"Main parameters","kind":"kv","items":[{"label":"Frequency","value":"20–50 Hz (strengthening) · 1–10 Hz (endurance)"},{"label":"Pulse duration","value":"200–400 µs"},{"label":"Intensity","value":"Strong but comfortable contraction"},{"label":"Ramp","value":"1–2 s rise · 1–2 s fall"},{"label":"On : off time","value":"10:20 s (strength) · 5:10 s (endurance)"},{"label":"Treatment time","value":"10–20 min"}]},{"heading":"Indications","kind":"list","items":["Muscle weakness / atrophy","Post-operative muscle re-education","Disuse atrophy","Neuromuscular conditions","Pain relief (gate control theory)","DVT prevention (calf pump with FES)","Gait training, foot drop, hand grip, quadriceps activation"]},{"heading":"Clinical applications","kind":"list","items":["Quadriceps strengthening (post-injury / surgery)","Foot drop correction (FES)","Grip training (FES)","Calf pump (DVT prevention)","Pain management"]},{"heading":"Contraindications","kind":"list","tone":"warn","items":["Pacemaker","Malignancy","Pregnancy (abdomen & lumbosacral)","Epilepsy","Active TB","Over heart, eyes, testes","Open wounds / infections","Metal implants in region","Loss of sensation","Avoid carotid sinus & cervical region"]},{"heading":"Quick recall","kind":"list","items":["Correct electrode placement is essential","Keep skin clean and dry","Use proper electrode size","Increase intensity gradually","Comfort + effective contraction = the goal"]}]}],"PARAMETERS_TABLE":{"columns":["Modality","Frequency","Pulse duration","Intensity","Treatment time","Key uses"],"rows":[["TENS (conventional)","80–150 Hz","50–100 µs","Strong but comfortable (sensory)","20–30 min","Pain relief (gate control) · Acute & chronic pain · Muscle relaxation"],["Therapeutic ultrasound","1 MHz (3–5 cm) · 3 MHz (1–2 cm)","Continuous or pulsed (20%)","0.5–2.0 W/cm²","5–10 min","Pain relief · Tissue healing · Extensibility ↑"],["SWD","27.12 MHz","Continuous","High but comfortable (deep heating)","15–20 min","Deep heating · Pain relief · Promotes healing"],["IFT","Carrier 4,000 Hz · Beat 0–150 Hz","200–400 µs","Strong but comfortable","15–20 min","Pain relief · Muscle relaxation · Circulation ↑"],["Cryotherapy","—","—","Ice until mild numbness (tolerance)","10–20 min","Pain relief · Inflammation ↓ · Acute injuries"],["Heat therapy","—","—","Tolerable heat","15–20 min","Pain relief · Blood flow ↑ · Relaxation & healing"],["NMES","20–50 Hz (strength) · 1–10 Hz (endurance)","200–400 µs","Strong, visible contraction","10–20 min","Strengthening · Re-education · Atrophy prevention"],["FES","35–50 Hz","250–400 µs","Strong, functional contraction","15–30 min","Restore function · Gait & ADL improvement · Neuromuscular re-education"]],"reminders":["Higher frequency → sensory nerve stimulation (pain relief)","Lower frequency → motor nerve stimulation (muscle contraction)","Set intensity to patient tolerance — never painful","Always consider contraindications"],"flow":["Assess","Choose","Apply","Reassess"],"flowNote":"Right modality + right parameters = better result, faster recovery."}},"electrotherapyEvidence":{"EVIDENCE_MODALITIES":[{"id":"eswt","name":"Shockwave (ESWT)","accent":"#f97316","records":6000,"strength":"Strong","depth":{"from":1,"to":5},"mechanism":"Acoustic pressure waves (0.05–0.4 mJ/mm²) create mechanical stress that drives neovascularisation, collagen remodelling and reduced substance-P signalling.","bestEvidence":["Plantar fasciitis and calcific rotator-cuff tendinopathy — repeated RCTs and meta-analyses show clinically meaningful pain reduction versus sham","Greater trochanteric pain and patellar / Achilles tendinopathy — moderate evidence, best combined with progressive loading","Typical dosing: 3–5 weekly sessions, 2,000–2,500 impulses per session"],"caution":"Avoid over lung tissue, large vessels, growth plates, and in coagulopathy or pregnancy.","term":"extracorporeal shockwave therapy tendinopathy"},{"id":"tens","name":"TENS","accent":"#22d3ee","records":5000,"strength":"Moderate","depth":{"from":0,"to":1.5},"mechanism":"Sensory-level pulses recruit large-diameter A-beta fibres (gate control) and, at low frequency / high intensity, endogenous opioid pathways.","bestEvidence":["Short-term pain relief in acute and chronic musculoskeletal pain — consistent but modest effect sizes","Effect is intensity-dependent: a strong, non-painful sensation outperforms low-intensity settings","Tolerance develops — vary frequency and electrode site across sessions"],"caution":"No electrodes over the anterior neck, transcerebrally, or near a pacemaker.","term":"transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation pain relief"},{"id":"nmes","name":"NMES","accent":"#4ade80","records":4000,"strength":"Strong","depth":{"from":1,"to":2.5},"mechanism":"Motor-level stimulation depolarises motor axons, recruiting fast-twitch fibres first — the reverse of voluntary recruitment order.","bestEvidence":["Quadriceps strength after ACL reconstruction and knee arthroplasty — added to exercise it beats exercise alone","Counteracts disuse atrophy during immobilisation and ICU stays","Effective doses use high training intensity, 10–15 contractions, on:off around 1:3–1:5"],"caution":"Contraindicated over a pacemaker or ICD, and in uncontrolled epilepsy or active malignancy in the field.","term":"neuromuscular electrical stimulation quadriceps strength"},{"id":"ultrasound","name":"Therapeutic ultrasound","accent":"#a78bfa","records":4000,"strength":"Limited","depth":{"from":1,"to":5},"mechanism":"Thermal effects (raised tissue temperature, extensibility) plus non-thermal cavitation and acoustic streaming at low duty cycles.","bestEvidence":["Heavily researched, but reviews find small and inconsistent benefit over sham for most musculoskeletal pain","Most defensible use is heating deeper tissue before manual therapy or stretch, not as a stand-alone treatment","1 MHz for 3–5 cm depth, 3 MHz for 1–2 cm; move the head continuously over 2× the applicator area"],"caution":"Not over growth plates, malignancy, thrombosis, eyes, or a pregnant uterus.","term":"therapeutic ultrasound musculoskeletal randomized"},{"id":"tecar","name":"TECAR / radiofrequency diathermy","accent":"#facc15","records":150,"strength":"Emerging","depth":{"from":2,"to":6},"mechanism":"Radiofrequency current (roughly 0.3–1.2 MHz) moves charges in the tissue itself — capacitive mode heats superficial, resistive mode heats deep and bony interfaces.","bestEvidence":["Small RCTs and pilot trials report short-term pain and range-of-motion gains in low back pain and tendinopathy","Evidence base is far smaller than shockwave, TENS or NMES — treat effects as short-term and adjunctive","Usually combined with movement or manual therapy during the session, which confounds the isolated effect"],"caution":"Same heat cautions as diathermy: no metal implants in the field, no impaired sensation, no acute inflammation.","term":"capacitive resistive electric transfer TECAR therapy"},{"id":"ift","name":"Interferential current","accent":"#60a5fa","records":800,"strength":"Moderate","depth":{"from":2,"to":4},"mechanism":"Two medium-frequency currents (≈4,000 Hz) interfere to produce a low-frequency beat deep in the tissue, bypassing skin impedance.","bestEvidence":["Comparable short-term analgesia to TENS with better comfort at depth","Best supported as part of a package with exercise rather than alone"],"caution":"Standard electrical-stimulation contraindications apply.","term":"interferential current therapy pain"},{"id":"cryotherapy","name":"Cryotherapy","accent":"#38bdf8","records":3000,"strength":"Moderate","depth":{"from":1,"to":3},"mechanism":"Cooling reduces nerve conduction velocity, metabolic rate and blood flow — analgesia dominates the measurable effect.","bestEvidence":["Reliable short-term analgesia after acute injury and surgery","Intramuscular cooling depends on fat thickness — thicker fat means far less deep cooling","Prolonged icing may blunt some remodelling signals; use for pain control, not as the whole plan"],"caution":"Avoid with cold hypersensitivity, Raynaud's, or impaired sensation.","term":"cryotherapy acute soft tissue injury"}],"MAX_RECORDS":6000},"eliteSCData":{"elitePrinciples":[{"id":"beyond-barbell","num":"01","title":"Value Beyond the Barbell","tag":"Diminishing Returns","summary":"At the elite level, adding more raw strength stops moving the needle on the field.","sections":[{"heading":"The Elite Strength Threshold","text":"On-field performance yield rises steeply in the early years of strength training, then flattens. Adding 10 lb to a 400 lb squat rarely dictates on-field success."},{"heading":"Where the coach's value shifts","points":["Clearing acute movement limitations","Managing body structure constraints","Ensuring the athlete can reliably replicate their delivery"]}]},{"id":"sc-vs-performance","num":"02","title":"S&C vs Performance Coach","tag":"Paradigm Shift","summary":"Two mindsets: chasing weight-room numbers vs owning athlete availability and skill delivery.","sections":[{"heading":"The traditional S&C coach","points":["Barbell metrics are the yardstick — success means the weight goes up","Blind spots form around anything the barbell can't measure"]},{"heading":"The high-performance coach","points":["Athlete availability is the first metric","Tissue health is actively managed, not assumed","Skill delivery — health, mechanics, arm/limb trajectory — is the end product"]}]},{"id":"golden-egg","num":"03","title":"The 'Golden Egg' of Asymmetrical Sports","tag":"Risk Threshold","summary":"Elite rotational athletes need a specific level of postural adaptation — up to a natural threshold.","sections":[{"heading":"The natural threshold","text":"Outcome rises with postural asymmetry and adaptation through a performance benefit zone — then tips into an injury risk zone past the threshold. The defining challenge of S&C is finding the exact point where performance peaks before injury risk spikes."},{"heading":"Why it matters","text":"“If a pitcher throws 110 mph, that's a huge performance benefit. But if they get hurt after one inning, they are useless to the team.”"}]},{"id":"asymmetrical-athlete","num":"04","title":"Programming for the Asymmetrical Athlete","tag":"Counter-Stimulus","summary":"Dominant-side overuse shifts range of motion and posture — balance it with low-dose opposite work.","sections":[{"heading":"The dominant side","text":"Athletes naturally over-rotate and overuse their dominant side (e.g. right-handed throwers constantly rotating left). This shifts workable range of motion and creates extreme postural asymmetry."},{"heading":"The counter-stimulus","text":"Program low-dose opposite-direction rotation (e.g. opposite med-ball throws) to remind the nervous system of balanced mechanics."},{"heading":"Key clinical benefits","points":["Mitigates overuse injuries and runaway postural shifting","Reconnects the athlete's mind-body neurological system","Firms up foundational, bilateral movement patterns"]}]},{"id":"rotational-dose","num":"05","title":"Modulating the Rotational Dose","tag":"Season Planning","summary":"The rotational training dose flips between in-season maintenance and off-season building.","sections":[{"heading":"In-season workload","points":["Avoid programming heavy rotational volume in the weight room — practice and competition already provide excessive primary stimulus","Program low-dose opposite-direction rotation to restore baseline symmetry and remind the CNS of balanced mechanics"]},{"heading":"Off-season protocol","points":["Deploy a balanced, aggressive approach — rotate heavy in both dominant and non-dominant directions","Focus entirely on general athleticism, high-force absolute production, and multidirectional pelvic & t-spine mobility"]}]},{"id":"bilateral-unilateral","num":"06","title":"The Bilateral → Unilateral Evolution","tag":"Training Maturity","summary":"Training evolves from two-legged barbell strength toward single-side positions that mirror sport.","sections":[{"heading":"1 · The bilateral foundation","text":"The X's and O's of the weight room: heavy squats, deadlifts and bench press build peak force output. Motivation is often driven by a fear of seeing weight-room numbers drop."},{"heading":"2 · The maturation phase","text":"Observation dictates reality — recognising distinct body structures, strict mobility limitations and individual kinematic profiles."},{"heading":"3 · Elite level: unilateral dominance","text":"The shift to reverse lunges, step-ups and split squats — placing athletes in realistic, asymmetrical positions that directly replicate and maximise on-field performance translation."}]},{"id":"translation-gap","num":"07","title":"Bridging the Translation Gap","tag":"Lab → Field","summary":"Perfect lab science only matters once it survives the messy reality of the field.","sections":[{"heading":"The lab (academia)","text":"PhD research, highly controlled environments, isolated variables and perfect theoretical biomechanical ideals."},{"heading":"The field (reality)","text":"Organised chaos, immense environmental factors, unpredictable game stress and systemic travel fatigue."},{"heading":"The translation","text":"The best practitioners filter perfect laboratory ideals through the messy reality of the weight room. Improving lab explosiveness only matters if the athlete can still practise their sport."}]},{"id":"signal-over-noise","num":"08","title":"Tech Integration: Signal Over Noise","tag":"Sports Tech","summary":"Force plates, ball-flight and motion capture are only valuable when the streams connect.","sections":[{"heading":"The streams","points":["Force plates — ground reaction vectors","Ball-flight tracking — flight and spin rates","Motion capture — kinematic sequencing"]},{"heading":"The noise","text":"The danger of modern sports science is getting lost in isolated, siloed metrics without physiological context."},{"heading":"The correlation (signal)","text":"True value lies in connecting the streams — e.g. correlating specific back-leg force vectors directly to quantifiable increases in ball spin rate."}]},{"id":"elite-schedule","num":"09","title":"The Reality of the Elite Schedule","tag":"Travel & Fatigue","summary":"Programming cannot exist in a vacuum — it must adapt to massive systemic travel fatigue.","sections":[{"heading":"The 162+ game grind","text":"Athletes compete at peak intensity nearly every single day for months on end."},{"heading":"The travel toll","text":"Finishing a 7:00 PM Sunday night game, packing the clubhouse, flying cross-country, and arriving at 5:00 AM."},{"heading":"CNS drain","text":"Bouncing across multiple time zones every 3–4 days drastically suppresses the central nervous system and obliterates natural recovery cycles. Weight-room programming must dynamically adapt to this fatigue."}]},{"id":"human-element","num":"10","title":"The Human Element of High Performance","tag":"Lifestyle","summary":"Sustaining a career in professional sport requires intentional lifestyle management.","sections":[{"heading":"Career + life","text":"Navigating a relentless travel schedule alongside a family demands constant evolution and adaptability — performance is built at home as much as in the gym."},{"heading":"The practitioner's evolution","text":"Moving from a chaotic phase of constant travel toward establishing a permanent home base — and using brief off-seasons to rapidly transition back into the primary roles of spouse and parent."}]}]},"frameworkData":{"frameworkOverview":{"title":"THE HIGH PERFORMANCE FRAMEWORK","subtitle":"Seven pillars of sport science, grounded in peer-reviewed research","description":"A synthesis of the literature on athletic performance — simplified into actionable principles. Each pillar is backed by key references from the sport science canon.","tagline":"TRAIN SMART. RECOVER HARDER. MEASURE EVERYTHING."},"frameworkPillars":[{"id":"periodization","title":"Periodization & Load Management","icon":"CalendarRange","color":"#00d4ff","summary":"Systematic variation of training stimuli to peak at the right time while managing fatigue.","keyPoints":["Alternating stress and recovery drives supercompensation","Acute:chronic workload ratio (ACWR) of 0.8–1.3 reduces injury risk","Progressive overload must respect tissue adaptation timelines"],"references":[{"authors":"Gabbett, T. J.","year":"2016","title":"The training—injury prevention paradox: should athletes be training smarter and harder?","journal":"British Journal of Sports Medicine","vol":"50(5)"},{"authors":"Banister, E. W.","year":"1991","title":"Modeling elite athletic performance","journal":"Physiological Testing of the High-Performance Athlete","vol":"Ch. 18"}]},{"id":"recovery","title":"Recovery & Adaptation","icon":"Moon","color":"#a78bfa","summary":"Adaptation occurs during recovery, not training. Sleep is the most powerful recovery tool available.","keyPoints":["Less than 8 hours sleep increases injury risk by 70%","Sleep extension improves sprint time, reaction time, and accuracy","Cold water immersion may blunt hypertrophy adaptations if used post-training"],"references":[{"authors":"Milewski, M. D., et al.","year":"2014","title":"Chronic lack of sleep is associated with increased sports injuries in youth athletes","journal":"Journal of Pediatric Orthopaedics","vol":"34(2)"},{"authors":"Mah, C. D., et al.","year":"2011","title":"The effects of sleep extension on athletic performance: a pilot study","journal":"Sleep","vol":"34(7)"},{"authors":"Roberts, L. A., et al.","year":"2015","title":"Post-exercise cold water immersion attenuates muscle adaptation","journal":"FASEB Journal","vol":"29(4)"}]},{"id":"neuromuscular","title":"Neuromuscular Training","icon":"Zap","color":"#fbbf24","summary":"Neural adaptations precede structural changes. Rate of force development is the critical variable.","keyPoints":["Neural adaptations account for early strength gains (first 4–6 weeks)","Explosive strength training improves rate of force development","Eccentric training increases tendon stiffness and reduces injury risk"],"references":[{"authors":"Moritani, T., & deVries, H. A.","year":"1979","title":"Neural factors vs. hypertrophy in the time course of muscle strength gain","journal":"American Journal of Physical Medicine","vol":"58(3)"},{"authors":"Cormie, P., et al.","year":"2011","title":"Developing maximal neuromuscular power: biological basis of maximal power production","journal":"Sports Medicine","vol":"41(1)"},{"authors":"Douglas, J., et al.","year":"2017","title":"Chronic adaptation to eccentric training: a review of the literature","journal":"Journal of Athletic Enhancement","vol":"6(1)"}]},{"id":"biomechanics","title":"Biomechanics & Movement Quality","icon":"Move","color":"#34d399","summary":"Movement quality determines load distribution. Poor mechanics concentrate stress; good mechanics distribute it.","keyPoints":["Landing mechanics predict non-contact ACL injury risk","Asymmetries >15% in bilateral strength increase injury risk","Fascial tensioning transmits force across the kinetic chain"],"references":[{"authors":"Hewett, T. E., et al.","year":"2005","title":"Biomechanical measures of neuromuscular control and valgus loading of the knee predict ACL injury risk","journal":"American Journal of Sports Medicine","vol":"33(4)"},{"authors":"Croisier, J. L., et al.","year":"2008","title":"Strength imbalances and prevention of hamstring injury in professional soccer players","journal":"American Journal of Sports Medicine","vol":"36(8)"},{"authors":"Schleip, R., et al.","year":"2012","title":"Fascia is able to contract in a smooth muscle-like manner and thereby influence musculoskeletal mechanics","journal":"Journal of Biomechanics","vol":"45(8)"}]},{"id":"psychology","title":"Psychology & Mental Performance","icon":"Brain","color":"#f472b6","summary":"Mental readiness is trainable. Confidence, focus, and emotional regulation directly affect physical output.","keyPoints":["Self-talk and imagery improve motor skill acquisition and performance","Psychological resilience is a predictor of athletic success","Pre-competition anxiety management improves performance consistency"],"references":[{"authors":"Hardy, J., et al.","year":"2009","title":"Self-talk: a literature review","journal":"Journal of Sport and Exercise Psychology","vol":"31(6)"},{"authors":"Gucciardi, D. F., & Gordon, S.","year":"2011","title":"Mental toughness in sport: developments in theory and research","journal":"Routledge","vol":""},{"authors":"Jones, G., et al.","year":"2002","title":"What is this thing called mental toughness?","journal":"Journal of Applied Sport Psychology","vol":"14(1)"}]},{"id":"nutrition","title":"Nutrition & Hydration","icon":"Utensils","color":"#fb923c","summary":"Fuel availability directly affects training quality, recovery, and adaptation. Timing matters as much as amount.","keyPoints":["Protein intake of 1.6–2.2g/kg bodyweight maximizes muscle protein synthesis","Carbohydrate availability influences training adaptation and performance","Dehydration of >2% body mass impairs cognitive and physical performance"],"references":[{"authors":"Morton, R. W., et al.","year":"2018","title":"A systematic review and meta-analysis of protein intake to maximize muscle hypertrophy","journal":"British Journal of Sports Medicine","vol":"52(6)"},{"authors":"Burke, L. M., et al.","year":"2011","title":"Carbohydrates for training and competition","journal":"Journal of Sports Sciences","vol":"29(sup1)"},{"authors":"Sawka, M. N., et al.","year":"2007","title":"Exercise and fluid replacement","journal":"Medicine & Science in Sports & Exercise","vol":"39(2)"}]},{"id":"loadmonitoring","title":"Load Monitoring & Athlete Monitoring","icon":"Activity","color":"#60a5fa","summary":"You can't manage what you don't measure. Systematic monitoring of load, wellness, and readiness prevents overtraining.","keyPoints":["Session-RPE × duration is a valid, practical training load metric","Wellness questionnaires detect overreaching before performance declines","HRV monitoring reflects autonomic nervous system recovery status"],"references":[{"authors":"Foster, C., et al.","year":"2001","title":"A new approach to monitoring exercise training","journal":"Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research","vol":"15(1)"},{"authors":"Hooper, S. L., & Mackinnon, L. T.","year":"1995","title":"Monitoring overtraining in athletes","journal":"Sports Medicine","vol":"20(5)"},{"authors":"Plews, D. J., et al.","year":"2013","title":"Training adaptation and heart rate variability in elite endurance athletes","journal":"Sports Medicine","vol":"43(9)"}]}]},"clinicalDomainData":{"CLINICAL_REFERENCES":{"workflow":[{"title":"ACSM's Guidelines for Exercise Testing and Prescription, 12th Ed.","source":"ACSM (2022)","url":"https://www.acsm.org/education-resources/books/guidelines-exercise-testing-prescription"},{"title":"Updating ACSM's recommendations for exercise preparticipation health screening","source":"Riebe et al., MSSE (2015)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26473759/"},{"title":"Monitoring athlete training loads: consensus statement","source":"Bourdon et al., IJSPP (2017)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28463642/"}],"scoring":[{"title":"Measures of reliability in sports medicine and science","source":"Hopkins, Sports Med (2000)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10907753/"},{"title":"Progressive statistics for studies in sports medicine and exercise science","source":"Hopkins et al., MSSE (2009)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19092709/"},{"title":"Standardisation and methodological considerations for the countermovement jump test","source":"Comfort et al., Strength Cond J (2019)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37476719/"}],"norms":[{"title":"Normative reference values for the CPET / VO₂max (FRIEND registry)","source":"Kaminsky et al., Mayo Clin Proc (2015)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26455884/"},{"title":"Normal walking speed: a descriptive meta-analysis","source":"Bohannon & Williams Andrews, Physiotherapy (2011)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21820535/"},{"title":"On the analysis of movement smoothness (SPARC)","source":"Balasubramanian et al., JNER (2015)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26651329/"},{"title":"Interlimb asymmetries: understanding how to calculate, quantify and interpret them","source":"Bishop et al., Strength Cond J (2018)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33814818/"}],"prescription":[{"title":"Quantity and quality of exercise for developing and maintaining fitness","source":"Garber et al., MSSE (2011)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21694556/"},{"title":"Progression models in resistance training for healthy adults","source":"ACSM position stand, MSSE (2009)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19204579/"},{"title":"Tapering practices and performance: a meta-analysis","source":"Bosquet et al., MSSE (2007)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17596787/"},{"title":"New horizons for the methodology and physiology of training periodization","source":"Issurin, Sports Med (2010)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20199120/"}],"equipment":[{"title":"Inertial measurement units for clinical movement analysis: reliability and concurrent validity","source":"Al-Amri et al., Sensors (2018)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29558425/"},{"title":"Development of recommendations for SEMG sensors and sensor placement (SENIAM)","source":"Hermens et al., J Electromyogr Kinesiol (2000)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11018445/"},{"title":"The present and future use of fNIRS for cognitive neuroscience","source":"Pinti et al., Ann NY Acad Sci (2020)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30256433/"},{"title":"ATS/ACCP statement on cardiopulmonary exercise testing","source":"Am J Respir Crit Care Med (2003)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12524257/"}],"risk":[{"title":"Exercise preparticipation health screening recommendations — scientific statement","source":"Riebe et al., MSSE (2017)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28650869/"},{"title":"Clinical recommendations for cardiopulmonary exercise testing data assessment","source":"Guazzi et al., Circulation (2016)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27143685/"},{"title":"AHA/ACC scientific statement: exercise standards for testing and training","source":"Fletcher et al., Circulation (2013)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23877260/"}]},"CONTRAINDICATIONS":[{"kind":"Absolute — do not test","color":"#f87171","items":["Acute myocardial infarction within 3–5 days","Unstable angina","Uncontrolled arrhythmia causing symptoms or haemodynamic compromise","Active endocarditis, acute myocarditis or pericarditis","Symptomatic severe aortic stenosis","Decompensated heart failure","Acute pulmonary embolism, infarction or deep-vein thrombosis","Acute aortic dissection","Physical disability precluding safe and adequate testing"]},{"kind":"Relative — test only with clinician sign-off","color":"#fbbf24","items":["Known obstructive left main coronary stenosis","Moderate–severe aortic stenosis of uncertain symptom status","Tachy- or bradyarrhythmias, including uncontrolled atrial fibrillation","Acquired advanced or complete heart block","Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy","Uncontrolled hypertension (SBP >200 or DBP >110 mmHg)","Uncorrected medical condition — anaemia, electrolyte imbalance, thyrotoxicosis","Acute musculoskeletal injury or post-operative restriction affecting the test"]}],"DOMAIN_WEIGHTS":[{"domain":"Strength","color":"#4ade80","default":25,"rehab":30,"performance":30,"healthspan":20},{"domain":"Movement","color":"#22d3ee","default":25,"rehab":40,"performance":25,"healthspan":25},{"domain":"Cardiovascular","color":"#fb923c","default":25,"rehab":15,"performance":30,"healthspan":40},{"domain":"Cognitive","color":"#a78bfa","default":25,"rehab":15,"performance":15,"healthspan":15}],"EQUIPMENT":[{"domain":"Strength","device":"STRENGTHSenses GRFP force plates","measures":"CMJ, SJ, IMTP, RSI, force symmetry index","units":"N · N/kg · N/s · N·s · m/s","validity":"1000 Hz sampling · body-weight zeroing before each trial · typical error 2–4% for CMJ (Comfort et al. 2019)"},{"domain":"Strength","device":"Hand grip dynamometer","measures":"Grip strength, absolute + relative","units":"kg · kg/kg","validity":"Standardised elbow at 90°, best of 3 per hand · ICC >0.95 (Roberts et al. 2011)"},{"domain":"Strength","device":"Proteus 2.0","measures":"3D dynamic power, force–velocity profile","units":"W · N","validity":"Calibrate monthly · 5-point force–velocity profile, R² ≥0.95 accepted (Samozino et al. 2016)"},{"domain":"Strength","device":"LIFTER","measures":"Functional lifting force, bar velocity","units":"N · m/s","validity":"Velocity loss cut-off 10–20% per set · error ~0.03 m/s vs linear encoder"},{"domain":"Movement","device":"Noraxon Ultium 3D","measures":"Joint angles, SPARC smoothness, segment velocity","units":"° · SPARC · m/s","validity":"Static + dynamic calibration each session · angle error <5° vs optoelectronic (Al-Amri et al. 2018)"},{"domain":"Movement","device":"Medilogic insoles","measures":"Ground contact time, cadence, stride, loading asymmetry","units":"ms · steps/min · m","validity":"Body-weight calibration walk · ICC 0.87–0.96 for spatiotemporal gait (Braun et al. 2015)"},{"domain":"Movement","device":"NiNox cameras (2D)","measures":"Stride length, step width, 2D joint kinematics","units":"m · °","validity":"Fixed camera height + scaling reference in frame · 2D valid for sagittal plane only"},{"domain":"Movement","device":"Noraxon Ultium EMG","measures":"Muscle onset timing, co-contraction index, amplitude","units":"ms · %MVC","validity":"Skin prep + MVC normalisation per muscle · SENIAM electrode placement (Hermens et al. 2000)"},{"domain":"Cognitive","device":"DYNAVISION D2","measures":"Simple RT, dual-task RT, dual-task cost, peripheral accuracy","units":"ms · %","validity":"One familiarisation trial required — learning effect ~7% on first exposure (Blackwell et al. 2020)"},{"domain":"Cognitive","device":"NIH Toolbox (tablet)","measures":"Processing speed, executive function, working memory","units":"standard score","validity":"Age-, sex- and education-corrected norms · test–retest ICC 0.78–0.90 (Weintraub et al. 2013)"},{"domain":"Cognitive","device":"Artinis Brite MKIV fNIRS","measures":"Prefrontal ΔHbO, ΔHbR, cognitive load index","units":"µM","validity":"Signal-quality check per channel; motion-artefact rejection before scoring (Pinti et al. 2020)"},{"domain":"Cardiovascular","device":"CPET bike + VO₂ cart","measures":"VO₂max, VT1, VT2, RER, work rate at threshold","units":"mL/kg/min · W","validity":"Gas + volume calibration before every test · maximal criteria: RER ≥1.10, plateau, RPE ≥18 (ATS/ACCP 2003)"},{"domain":"Cardiovascular","device":"HR monitor (CPET)","measures":"HRmax, HR at VT1/VT2, HR recovery, submaximal HR","units":"bpm","validity":"Chest strap; wrist optical not accepted above VT1 · seated 1-min recovery protocol"}],"NORMATIVE_METRICS":[{"metric":"VO₂max","unit":"mL/kg/min","ranges":{"sedentary":[18,33],"trained":[32,52],"competitive":[46,66],"elite":[58,90]},"source":"ACSM 12th Ed.; Squeo et al. (2025)","domain":"Cardiovascular"},{"metric":"VT1","unit":"mL/kg/min","ranges":{"sedentary":[11,20],"trained":[20,32],"competitive":[28,44],"elite":[38,62]},"source":"Buchheit & Laursen (2013)","domain":"Cardiovascular"},{"metric":"VT2 / RCP","unit":"% of VO₂max","ranges":{"sedentary":[65,75],"trained":[75,83],"competitive":[80,88],"elite":[85,93]},"source":"Meyer et al. (2005); Guazzi et al. (2016)","domain":"Cardiovascular"},{"metric":"HR recovery, 1 min","unit":"bpm","ranges":{"sedentary":[10,22],"trained":[20,36],"competitive":[28,48],"elite":[38,70]},"source":"Cole et al. (1999) NEJM","domain":"Cardiovascular"},{"metric":"CMJ peak force","unit":"N/kg","ranges":{"sedentary":[12,19],"trained":[16,22],"competitive":[19,26],"elite":[22,30]},"source":"Linthorne (2001); McMahon et al. (2018)","domain":"Strength"},{"metric":"IMTP peak force","unit":"N/kg","ranges":{"sedentary":[10,19],"trained":[16,24],"competitive":[21,31],"elite":[28,48]},"source":"Dos'Santos et al. (2017); Haff et al. (2015)","domain":"Strength"},{"metric":"RSI, drop jump","unit":"m/s","ranges":{"sedentary":[0.3,0.9],"trained":[0.8,1.6],"competitive":[1.3,2.1],"elite":[1.8,3.2]},"source":"Flanagan & Comyns (2008); Beattie et al. (2017)","domain":"Strength"},{"metric":"Grip strength","unit":"kg","ranges":{"sedentary":[22,38],"trained":[32,50],"competitive":[40,56],"elite":[48,66]},"source":"ACSM; Norman et al. (2011)","domain":"Strength"},{"metric":"Limb symmetry index","unit":"% (higher is better)","ranges":{"sedentary":[82,92],"trained":[88,95],"competitive":[90,97],"elite":[93,99]},"source":"Bishop et al. (2018) Strength Cond J","domain":"Strength"},{"metric":"Gait velocity, habitual","unit":"m/s","ranges":{"sedentary":[0.9,1.25],"trained":[1.25,1.45],"competitive":[1.35,1.55],"elite":[1.4,1.65]},"source":"Bohannon & Andrews (2011)","domain":"Movement"},{"metric":"Cadence","unit":"steps/min","ranges":{"sedentary":[95,110],"trained":[108,122],"competitive":[116,130],"elite":[124,138]},"source":"Tudor-Locke et al. (2018)","domain":"Movement"},{"metric":"Ankle dorsiflexion ROM","unit":"° (weight-bearing)","ranges":{"sedentary":[18,30],"trained":[28,38],"competitive":[32,42],"elite":[35,48]},"source":"Bennell et al. (1998); Hoch & McKeon (2011)","domain":"Movement"},{"metric":"Shoulder flexion ROM","unit":"°","ranges":{"sedentary":[140,165],"trained":[160,175],"competitive":[165,180],"elite":[170,185]},"source":"Norkin & White (2016) goniometry","domain":"Movement"},{"metric":"SPARC smoothness","unit":"arb. (less negative is better)","ranges":{"sedentary":[-2.2,-1.8],"trained":[-1.9,-1.6],"competitive":[-1.75,-1.5],"elite":[-1.65,-1.4]},"source":"Balasubramanian et al. (2015) JNER","domain":"Movement"},{"metric":"Loading asymmetry, gait","unit":"% (lower is better)","invert":true,"ranges":{"sedentary":[8,18],"trained":[5,12],"competitive":[3,8],"elite":[1,5]},"source":"Medilogic norms; Zifchock et al. (2008)","domain":"Movement"},{"metric":"Reaction time, D2","unit":"ms (lower is better)","invert":true,"ranges":{"sedentary":[380,580],"trained":[270,380],"competitive":[215,275],"elite":[162,218]},"source":"Blackwell et al. (2020) AJOT","domain":"Cognitive"},{"metric":"Dual-task cost","unit":"% (lower is better)","invert":true,"ranges":{"sedentary":[22,45],"trained":[12,28],"competitive":[7,18],"elite":[3,10]},"source":"Strobach et al. (2012); Blackwell et al. (2020)","domain":"Cognitive"},{"metric":"Processing speed","unit":"standard score","ranges":{"sedentary":[38,58],"trained":[46,67],"competitive":[55,76],"elite":[62,95]},"source":"NIH Toolbox CB V3 (2025)","domain":"Cognitive"}],"PERIODIZATION_MODELS":[{"name":"Linear (LP)","for":["sedentary","trained"],"detail":"Progressive ↑ intensity, ↓ volume. 52-week macrocycle: anatomical adaptation → hypertrophy → strength.","source":"Bompa & Haff (2009), NSCA"},{"name":"Daily Undulating (DUP)","for":["trained","competitive"],"detail":"Daily rotation of rep ranges — hypertrophy / strength / power. Prevents accommodation. 16-week macrocycle.","source":"Rhea et al. (2002); Zourdos et al. (2016)"},{"name":"Block (Issurin)","for":["competitive","elite"],"detail":"Sequential blocks: accumulation → transmutation → realisation. Minimises interference. 20-week macrocycle.","source":"Issurin (2010); Kiely (2012)"},{"name":"Conjugate (Westside)","for":["competitive","elite"],"detail":"Concurrent max-effort and dynamic-effort days, ME/DE lower + upper rotated weekly.","source":"Simmons (1999); Zatsiorsky & Kraemer (2006)"},{"name":"Triphasic (Dietz)","for":["elite"],"detail":"Sequential eccentric → isometric → concentric blocks. Maximises neuromuscular efficiency. 12-week macrocycle.","source":"Dietz & Peterson (2012)"},{"name":"Bondarchuk","for":["elite"],"detail":"GPP → SPP → competition prep → competition. Long accumulation, Soviet sport science origin.","source":"Bondarchuk (2007); Verkhoshansky (1988)"}],"POPULATIONS":[{"id":"sedentary","label":"Sedentary","color":"#94a3b8","anchor":"5/10 = functional standard","description":"Inactive adults, ≤2 sessions/week. Focus: functional capacity, ADL, fall-risk.","references":"ACSM 12th Ed.; Rikli & Jones (2013)","prescription":{"cv":"3–5 d/wk · 40–59% HRR · 20–30 min · walking / cycling","resistance":"2–3 d/wk · 40–60% 1RM · machine-based","flexibility":"≥2–3 d/wk · static holds 10–30 s ×2–4 · to mild discomfort","neuromotor":"2–3 d/wk · 20–30 min balance / gait / agility — fall-risk priority","progression":"Volume first: +5–10 min per session every 1–2 wk for 4–6 wk, then intensity.","deload":"Every 4th week at ~50% volume; any 2 consecutive days of soreness ≥7/10 triggers a step back.","periodization":"Linear","notes":"Screen ACSM risk factors before vigorous exercise."}},{"id":"trained","label":"Trained","color":"#22d3ee","anchor":"7/10 = recreational standard","description":"Recreationally active, 3–5 sessions/week. Performance–health continuum.","references":"ACSM; Kenney et al. (2020); McMahon et al. (2018)","prescription":{"cv":"4–5 d/wk · 60–79% HRR · 30–60 min · running / cycling / rowing","resistance":"3–4 d/wk · 67–85% 1RM · free weights","flexibility":"Daily dynamic pre-session · static 2–3 d/wk post-session","neuromotor":"2 d/wk · plyometric and multi-directional drills, 80–120 contacts","progression":"Progressive overload 2.5–5% every 1–2 weeks; ACWR held at 0.8–1.3.","deload":"Deload week every 4–6 weeks at 40–60% volume.","periodization":"DUP or WUP","notes":"Progressive overload 2.5–5% every 1–2 weeks."}},{"id":"competitive","label":"Competitive","color":"#a78bfa","anchor":"7.5/10 = competitive standard","description":"Competitive athletes, structured sport-specific programming, regional–national level.","references":"NSCA 4th Ed.; Dos'Santos et al. (2017); Buchheit & Laursen (2013)","prescription":{"cv":"5–6 d/wk · Zone 2 (70%) + Zone 4–5 intervals ×2/wk","resistance":"4–5 d/wk · 75–95% 1RM · Olympic lifting","flexibility":"Daily dynamic warm-up · targeted mobility on restricted joints from testing","neuromotor":"3 d/wk · reactive plyometrics, COD mechanics, 120–200 contacts","progression":"Block-wise: accumulation +10% volume, transmutation +intensity at −20% volume.","deload":"Taper 7–10 days pre-competition: −40–60% volume, intensity maintained.","periodization":"Block or Conjugate","notes":"VT2-anchored intensification. Retest every 8 weeks."}},{"id":"elite","label":"Elite","color":"#fb923c","anchor":"8.5/10 = elite standard","description":"Elite / professional athletes, national–international level. Marginal gains logic.","references":"Haugen et al. (2020); Squeo et al. (2025); Buchheit (2014)","prescription":{"cv":"6–12 sessions/wk · polarised 80/20","resistance":"Triphasic / Bondarchuk · daily HRV + lactate monitoring","flexibility":"Individualised, driven by 3D kinematics and asymmetry flags","neuromotor":"Daily low-dose CNS priming; velocity-based cut-offs stop the set at −10% mean velocity","progression":"Force–velocity profile drives the next block; adjust weekly on HRV + CMJ trend.","deload":"Individualised taper (~2 weeks, −50% volume) with daily readiness monitoring.","periodization":"Triphasic, Block","notes":"Precision monitoring. Force–velocity profiling every 6 weeks."}}],"RISK_FACTORS":["Age ≥45 M / ≥55 F","Family history of CVD (1st-degree <55 M / <65 F)","Current smoker, or quit <6 months ago","Sedentary (<150 min/wk moderate PA)","Obesity (BMI ≥30, or waist >102 M / >88 F cm)","Hypertension (SBP ≥130 or DBP ≥80 mmHg)","Dyslipidemia (LDL ≥130 or HDL <40 mg/dL)","Pre-diabetes (fasting 100–125 mg/dL or HbA1c 5.7–6.4%)"],"RISK_SYMPTOMS":["Chest pain","Syncope","Dyspnoea","Orthopnoea","Ankle oedema","Palpitations","Claudication","Heart murmur","Unusual fatigue"],"RISK_TIERS":[{"tier":"LOW","color":"#4ade80","criteria":"0–1 risk factors, no symptoms, no known CVD / metabolic / renal disease.","action":"No medical clearance needed."},{"tier":"MODERATE","color":"#fbbf24","criteria":"≥2 risk factors.","action":"Medical clearance recommended before vigorous exercise."},{"tier":"HIGH","color":"#f87171","criteria":"Known CVD / metabolic / renal disease, or any symptom present.","action":"Medical clearance REQUIRED."}],"SCORE_BANDS":[{"band":"< 25th percentile","score":"≤ 3.5","label":"Below Expected","color":"#f87171"},{"band":"25–50th percentile","score":"4.0–5.5","label":"Functional","color":"#fbbf24"},{"band":"50–75th percentile","score":"6.0–7.5","label":"Good","color":"#4ade80"},{"band":"> 75th percentile","score":"8.0–10","label":"Excellent","color":"#22d3ee"}],"SCORE_LABELS":[{"range":"1–2","label":"Very Limited","color":"#f87171"},{"range":"3–4","label":"Below Expected","color":"#fb923c"},{"range":"5–6","label":"Functional","color":"#fbbf24"},{"range":"7–8","label":"Good","color":"#4ade80"},{"range":"9–10","label":"Excellent","color":"#22d3ee"}],"SCORING_NOTES":[{"heading":"Domain score","points":["Each domain — Strength, Movement, Cardiovascular, Cognitive — is scored 0–10.","Raw metrics map to the 0–10 scale through category-specific normative percentile bands.","Several metrics feed each domain via a weighted average; metrics closest to the assessment objective carry more weight (VO₂max > submaximal HR)."]},{"heading":"Composite overall score","points":["Weighted mean of the four domain scores, 25% each by default.","The clinician may re-weight domains for patient goals or sport demands."]},{"heading":"Confidence interval","points":["Modelled from the number of valid trials, inter-trial coefficient of variation and equipment signal-quality flags.","Range 0–1.0 · default 0.85–0.95 for a full test battery."]},{"heading":"Red flag logic","points":["Raised when a metric falls below the category minimum threshold, or drops more than 15% from the previous assessment.","HIGH = below the minimum clinical threshold · MODERATE = within 10% of the threshold."]},{"heading":"Worked example — CMJ, trained athlete","points":["Raw CMJ peak force 20.4 N/kg against the trained range 16–22 → 73rd percentile.","73rd percentile falls in the 50–75th band → domain contribution 7.4/10 (\"Good\").","Three valid trials with CV 4.1% → confidence 0.91; no red flag as the value sits above the category minimum."]},{"heading":"Real change vs noise","points":["A change counts as real only when it exceeds the smallest worthwhile change — typically 0.2 × between-athlete SD.","Typical error: CMJ 2–4%, IMTP 3–5%, VO₂max 3–5%, reaction time 5–8% — a within-error change is reported as stable.","Retest under matched conditions: same time of day, ≥24 h since hard training, identical warm-up."]}],"TERMINATION_CRITERIA":["Drop in SBP >10 mmHg below baseline despite rising work rate","Moderate–severe angina","Increasing nervous-system symptoms — ataxia, dizziness, near-syncope","Signs of poor perfusion — pallor, cyanosis, cold clammy skin","Sustained ventricular tachycardia or other significant arrhythmia","SBP >250 mmHg or DBP >115 mmHg","Technical failure of ECG, gas analysis or force-plate signal","Athlete asks to stop, or reports pain ≥7/10 in the tested region"],"WORKFLOW_STEPS":[{"n":1,"title":"Intake & Risk Screening","detail":"Identity verification, ACSM risk tier, informed consent, category confirmation.","output":"Risk tier + clearance decision","source":"Riebe et al. (2017) MSSE"},{"n":2,"title":"Questionnaire & Readiness","detail":"Sleep, fatigue, pain, training load, readiness to test, testing order.","output":"Readiness score; defer if pain ≥7/10","source":"Hooper & Mackinnon (1995)"},{"n":3,"title":"Movement Testing","detail":"ROM, gait velocity, stride length, cadence, reaction time, SPARC smoothness.","output":"Movement domain score + asymmetry index","source":"Balasubramanian et al. (2015) SPARC"},{"n":4,"title":"Strength Testing","detail":"CMJ peak force, RFD, impulse, RSI, IMTP, grip strength, symmetry index.","output":"Strength domain score","source":"Comfort et al. (2019); Haff et al. (2015)"},{"n":5,"title":"Cognitive Testing","detail":"NIH Toolbox processing speed / executive function, DYNAVISION D2 RT, dual-task cost, fNIRS.","output":"Cognitive domain score + dual-task cost","source":"Weintraub et al. (2013); Blackwell et al. (2020)"},{"n":6,"title":"Cardiovascular Testing","detail":"CPET VO₂max, VT1, VT2, HR recovery, RER, time to exhaustion.","output":"Cardiovascular domain score + training zones","source":"ATS/ACCP (2003); Guazzi et al. (2016)"},{"n":7,"title":"Scoring & Interpretation","detail":"Normative percentile model, composite domain scores, red flag review, confidence intervals.","output":"Composite 0–10 score + red flags","source":"Hopkins (2000) — measurement error"},{"n":8,"title":"Exercise Prescription (FITT-VP)","detail":"ACSM FITT-VP per domain and category, periodization model selection.","output":"Individualised FITT-VP plan","source":"ACSM 12th Ed. (2022); Garber et al. (2011)"},{"n":9,"title":"Education & Adherence","detail":"Topic assignment, patient portal activation, milestone goals, telehealth follow-up.","output":"Education plan + adherence targets","source":"Michie et al. (2013) BCT taxonomy"},{"n":10,"title":"PDF Report Generation","detail":"Clinical + patient-facing reports, audit trail, referring clinician distribution.","output":"Signed report + audit record","source":"APTA documentation standards"}]},"recoveryGuideData":{"DIAG":{"w":1,"d":0},"PRIORITY":{"1":{"label":"ESSENTIAL","color":"#1E9E56"},"2":{"label":"HELPFUL","color":"#BA7517"},"3":{"label":"OPTIONAL","color":"#6A6A66"}},"activityInfo":{"padel":{"sym":"🎾","name":"Padel","d":"Play each day — but let the colour guide how hard."},"weights":{"sym":"🏋️","name":"Weights session (with trainer)","d":"Your existing gym strength programme, led by your trainer — separate from foot rehab."},"proto":{"sym":"⚡","name":"7-minute protocol","d":"Red light, Compex and intrinsic foot exercises, all together."},"rehabstr":{"sym":"🦶","name":"Rehab strengthening (7 min)","d":"Foot and lower-leg rehab loading we set — separate from your gym weights."},"hand":{"sym":"🤝","name":"Hands-on therapy","d":"Keeps the ankle and calf moving well."},"gr":{"sym":"❄️","name":"Game Ready","d":"Cold and compression, 15–20 min, for comfort and swelling."},"rest":{"sym":"🌜","name":"Active rest","d":"Light movement only — let the foot recover."}},"dayNames":["Sun","Mon","Tue","Wed","Thu","Fri","Sat"],"diagnosis":{"lead":"A mild sprain of the spring ligament, on the inside of your right foot.","body":"This ligament works like a hammock under your arch, holding up the inside of your foot. Yours is strained and a little irritated — but there is no tear, so we treat it without surgery.","cards":[{"icon":"🦶","title":"Where","text":"Inner arch of the right foot, near the middle."},{"icon":"🔍","title":"The scan","text":"Mild sprain, no tear. Two nearby tendons slightly irritated — we watch them."},{"icon":"✅","title":"Good news","text":"Conservative treatment — no surgery needed."}]},"padelLoads":{"hard":{"cls":"hard","color":"#1E9E56","bg":"#D8F0DE","label":"Play hard","rpe":"RPE 8–9 / 10","d":"Full effort. Match play, competitive points, full sprints, cuts and overheads.","rule":"Fine to push today. Still stop if you get sharp inner-arch pain, or if the foot is more swollen or sore the next morning."},"med":{"cls":"med","color":"#BA7517","bg":"#FBEBD0","label":"Medium","rpe":"RPE 5–7 / 10","d":"Moderate effort. Rallies, controlled drills and shot practice — but ease off flat-out sprints, hard cutting and jumping.","rule":"Keep 2–3 points in reserve. Back off to easy if the arch starts to ache during play."},"easy":{"cls":"easy","color":"#A32D2D","bg":"#F8D7D7","label":"Go easy","rpe":"RPE 3–4 / 10","d":"Light effort only. Gentle hitting, technique and touch work, easy movement — no sprinting, hard cutting or jumping.","rule":"You are over your load threshold today. Stay well under pain. If it hurts, stop."}},"painOptions":[{"p":"1","icon":"🦶","n":"Arch support & awareness","short":"Offloading the ligament and re-learning to hold the arch.","detail":"A short daily routine to take strain off the spring ligament and wake up your own arch control, so the foot learns to support itself rather than collapsing inward. Longer-term support options like taping or an insole are something we will talk through separately.","dose":"10 minutes each morning, purely for offloading and arch awareness. Taping and insoles to be discussed."},{"p":"1","icon":"🤝","n":"Hands-on therapy","short":"Eases stiffness and helps you move normally.","detail":"Manual therapy from your physio — gentle joint mobilisation and soft-tissue work — keeps the ankle and calf moving well so you can walk, squat and land normally. Most effective when paired straight away with the exercise it unlocks.","dose":"3–4 times a week in the early phase, then taper to weekly or as needed in weeks 3–6. Always followed immediately by active exercise."},{"p":"2","icon":"❄️","n":"Compression + Ice Recovery boots","short":"Cooling and gentle pressure to ease swelling and pain.","detail":"A cold-compression system like the Game Ready wrap combines cooling and gentle pressure around the ankle. The cold calms pain and the compression helps ease swelling, so the foot feels and moves better — especially after a heavy session, standing, or travel. Comfort and recovery only, not a healing treatment.","dose":"15–20 min after activity or when swollen, most useful in weeks 1–3 while swelling settles."},{"p":"2","icon":"🌡️","n":"Contrast Therapy","short":"Alternating heat and cold to ease stiffness and swelling.","detail":"Contrast therapy alternates warm and cold to encourage circulation and ease stiffness and swelling, usually from the later early phase onward once there is no fresh, hot swelling. A comfort and recovery tool — it supports how you feel and move, it does not heal the ligament.","dose":"About 10–15 min total, alternating ~3–4 min warm with ~1 min cold, from roughly week 2 onward. A few times a week as needed."},{"p":"2","icon":"🔴","n":"Light therapy (red / near-infrared)","short":"May ease pain — does not heal the ligament by itself.","detail":"Red and near-infrared light may help with pain in some soft-tissue injuries. The evidence is mixed and it does not replace loading — so we use it as an optional add-on if it helps you feel better and train.","dose":"Around 10 min over the inner ankle at close range, 3–5 times a week through the early and mid phases."},{"p":"2","icon":"📡","n":"TECAR therapy (INDIBA)","short":"Deep warming radiofrequency for comfort and circulation.","detail":"TECAR (radiofrequency) gently warms the tissue at depth to ease pain and stiffness and support circulation. We use it as a comfort and mobility adjunct — handy before hands-on work or exercise, not as a substitute for loading.","dose":"Roughly 10–15 min over the medial ankle and calf, 1–3 times a week in the early-to-mid phases, ideally just before manual therapy or rehab."},{"p":"3","icon":"〰️","n":"Ultrasound therapy","short":"Optional — little proven benefit here.","detail":"Therapeutic ultrasound is sometimes offered, but the evidence for it in sprains like yours is weak. It is optional at best — we would rather put the effort into the essentials.","dose":"Not recommended as a priority. The essentials (loading, arch support) matter far more."}],"phaseColors":{"1":"#5DCAA5","2":"#EF9F27","3":"#0F6E56"},"phases":[{"id":1,"num":"1","label":"Calm it down","time":"Days 0–5","accent":"#5DCAA5","title":"Phase 1 — Inflammatory (calm it down)","feel":"Pain, swelling and warmth are normal here — they mean healing has started.","list":["We protect the foot and settle the swelling","Gentle movement keeps it from stiffening","Arch support goes on early"]},{"id":2,"num":"2","label":"Rebuild","time":"2 days – 6 weeks","accent":"#EF9F27","title":"Phase 2 — Repair (rebuild, protected)","feel":"New tissue forms, but it is still weak and can be re-injured. It may still feel tender.","list":["We start controlled, gradual loading","Building the new tissue the right way","Still protected — no full court loads yet"]},{"id":4,"num":"?","label":"Not improving?","time":"Check-in point","accent":"#8B6FC0","check":true,"title":"Not improving? Time to talk to your physician","feel":"If progress stalls between the rebuild and strength phases despite doing the rehab properly, this is a planned check-in — not a setback.","list":["We review your progress together and with your physician","We look at whether a scan or specialist opinion is needed","We discuss the next steps — which may include options like an injection if the foot is not responding","Nothing is rushed — this is a considered next step, only if needed"]},{"id":3,"num":"3","label":"Get strong","time":"6–12 weeks","accent":"#0F6E56","title":"Phase 3 — Remodeling (get strong)","feel":"The tissue slowly turns strong and organised as we load it. This is the longest phase.","list":["Progressive strength and impact work","This is what makes the foot padel-ready","Keeps building for months — patience pays off"]}],"protocolSpots":[{"id":"1","pos":{"left":30,"top":30},"color":"#6B4E9E","badge":"Muscle stimulation","title":"Compex on the calf & lower leg","text":"Pads sit over the calf and lower-leg muscles. The Compex makes them contract, doing strengthening work that supports the arch — useful early, when full-effort exercise is still limited."},{"id":"2","pos":{"left":68,"top":74},"color":"#A32D2D","badge":"Red light","title":"Red / near-infrared light on the foot","text":"Red-light panels sit close to the ankle and foot, running through the session to support recovery and ease discomfort. A comfort add-on — it does not replace loading."},{"id":"3","pos":{"left":55,"top":52},"color":"#0F6E56","badge":"Active control","title":"You drive the movement","text":"The key part: you are not lying still. You gently push into each stimulated contraction and control the foot, so the session is active loading — not a passive treatment. This is what makes it count."}],"rehabOptions":[{"p":"1","icon":"🦵","n":"Calf, arch & ankle strengthening","short":"Rebuilds the support around the ligament.","detail":"Strong calf, ankle and arch muscles take load off the ligament and are the core of your recovery. We build these gradually, from simple to single-leg to heavy.","dose":"3–4 days/week. Start with 3 sets of 12–15 in weeks 1–2, progress to single-leg and heavier loads (3–4 sets of 6–12) by weeks 5–6. Keep pain at 2/10 or less."},{"p":"1","icon":"🦶","n":"Intrinsic foot strengthening","short":"Wakes up the small muscles that support your arch.","detail":"The small muscles inside the foot are your arch's own support system. Training them helps hold the arch up and share the load the ligament was doing — a key, often-missed part of recovery.","dose":"Daily. Short-foot and toe exercises, 3 sets of 8–12 with brief holds. Progress from sitting to standing to single-leg."},{"p":"1","icon":"🧘","n":"Balance & control work","short":"Retrains the foot to stay stable when you move.","detail":"After a sprain the foot's sense of position is dulled. Balance work retrains it so you stay controlled when you move, land and cut — which protects against re-injury.","dose":"Most days, building to 4–5x/week. 3 rounds of 30–45 sec, progressing from steady single-leg stance to head turns, reaches, and controlled landings."},{"p":"1","icon":"🏃","n":"Gradual return to impact","short":"Hops, then cutting — builds full-function capacity.","detail":"We reintroduce impact in stages so the ligament and foot can handle padel's stop-start, cutting and push-off demands. Each step is checked before the next.","dose":"From weeks 3–4, ~2 days/week with 48 hours between. Start with low hops, build to bounds, deceleration and cutting by weeks 5–6. Progress only with no next-day flare."},{"p":"2","icon":"📈","n":"Load monitoring (wearable / insole)","short":"Tracks how much the foot is doing.","detail":"A wearable or smart insole can track how much load and how many hard movements the foot is taking, so we progress safely and spot when you are doing too much too soon.","dose":"Ongoing, whenever you train. Review weekly to keep load rising gradually and to catch left-right differences early."},{"p":"2","icon":"🚴","n":"Fitness maintenance (bike, pool)","short":"Keeps you fit while impact is limited.","detail":"While impact is limited, low-impact cardio keeps your fitness up so you are not starting from scratch when you return to full activity.","dose":"2–4 sessions/week of 20–40 min, at an easy-to-moderate effort, as long as it stays pain-free during and the next morning."},{"p":"2","icon":"⚡","n":"Compex device (muscle stimulation)","short":"Helps switch on and strengthen the muscles around the foot.","detail":"In muscle-stimulation (NMES) mode, the Compex sends stronger pulses that make the muscle contract. A useful add-on to wake up and build the calf and lower-leg muscles that support the arch — especially early on. It supports your strengthening; it does not replace it.","dose":"About 20 min, 3–4 days/week on the calf, at the strongest comfortable intensity. Most useful in the first few weeks; taper off as normal loading takes over."}],"severityLevels":[{"max":28,"key":"mild","marker":"Mild","accent":"#1D9E75","tag":"Mild sprain","title":"Timeline: ~2–4 weeks to settle, ~2–3 months to fully strong","text":"A straightforward mild sprain: pain calms within a few weeks and the ligament keeps strengthening for a couple of months. Yours sits just above this — a bit firmer.","pin":false},{"max":45,"key":"boundary","marker":"You are here","accent":"#BA7517","tag":"Higher-mild, edging into moderate — where you are","title":"Timeline: a few weeks to settle, closer to 3–4 months to fully strong","text":"Yours sits at the firmer end of mild, leaning toward moderate. The pain settles over the first few weeks, but the ligament needs a good few months to become truly strong. Expect a slightly longer, more careful build than a straightforward mild sprain — back to modified padel in weeks, full play in stages.","pin":true},{"max":66,"key":"mod","marker":"Moderate","accent":"#BA7517","tag":"Moderate — a larger partial tear","title":"Timeline: ~6–12 weeks, several months to fully strong","text":"A bigger partial tear needs several months of structured rehab before returning to impact. This is more than yours — shown so you can see the difference.","pin":false},{"max":101,"key":"sev","marker":"Severe","accent":"#A32D2D","tag":"Severe — full tear or unstable","title":"Timeline: 3–6+ months, often surgery","text":"A complete tear or an unstable foot usually means surgery and six months or more before returning to impact. This is far more serious than your injury.","pin":false}],"weeklySummary":[{"sym":"🎾","n":"7×","text":"Padel — every day, colour tells you how hard (green hard, orange medium, red easy)"},{"sym":"🏋️","n":"3×","text":"Weights session (with trainer) — your existing gym programme"},{"sym":"⚡","n":"3×","text":"7-minute protocol — red light, Compex and intrinsic foot exercises together"},{"sym":"🦶","n":"2×","text":"Rehab strengthening — a separate set of foot and lower-leg exercises"},{"sym":"🤝","n":"3×","text":"Hands-on therapy — to keep the ankle and calf moving well"},{"sym":"❄️","n":"2×","text":"Game Ready — cold and compression for comfort and swelling"},{"sym":"🌜","n":"1×","text":"Active rest day — light movement, let the foot recover"}],"weeks":[{"n":1,"phase":1,"plan":[["hand","gr"],["weights","proto"],["hand","rehabstr"],["proto"],["hand"],["weights","proto"],["rest","gr"]],"dates":["26 Jul","27 Jul","28 Jul","29 Jul","30 Jul","31 Jul","1 Aug"]},{"n":2,"phase":2,"plan":[["hand","gr"],["weights","proto"],["hand","rehabstr"],["proto","rehabstr"],["hand","rehabstr","gr"],["weights","proto"],["rest"]],"dates":["2 Aug","3 Aug","4 Aug","5 Aug","6 Aug","7 Aug","8 Aug"]},{"n":3,"phase":2,"plan":[["hand","gr"],["weights","proto"],["hand","rehabstr"],["weights","proto"],["hand","rehabstr","gr"],["weights","proto"],["rest"]],"dates":["9 Aug","10 Aug","11 Aug","12 Aug","13 Aug","14 Aug","15 Aug"]},{"n":4,"phase":2,"plan":[["hand","gr"],["weights","proto"],["hand","rehabstr"],["weights","proto"],["hand","rehabstr","gr"],["weights","proto"],["rest"]],"dates":["16 Aug","17 Aug","18 Aug","19 Aug","20 Aug","21 Aug","22 Aug"]},{"n":5,"phase":3,"plan":[["hand","gr"],["weights","proto"],["hand","rehabstr"],["weights","proto"],["hand","rehabstr","gr"],["weights","proto"],["rest"]],"dates":["23 Aug","24 Aug","25 Aug","26 Aug","27 Aug","28 Aug","29 Aug"]},{"n":6,"phase":3,"checkAt":1,"plan":[["hand","gr"],["weights","proto"],["hand","rehabstr"],["weights","proto"],["hand","rehabstr","gr"],["weights","proto"],["rest"]],"dates":["30 Aug","31 Aug","1 Sep","2 Sep","3 Sep","4 Sep","5 Sep"]},{"n":7,"phase":3,"plan":[["hand","gr"],["weights","proto"],["hand","rehabstr"],["weights","proto"],["hand","rehabstr","gr"],["weights","proto"],["rest"]],"dates":["6 Sep","7 Sep","8 Sep","9 Sep","10 Sep","11 Sep","12 Sep"]},{"n":8,"phase":3,"plan":[["hand","gr"],["weights","proto"],["hand","rehabstr"],["weights","proto"],["hand","rehabstr","gr"],["weights","proto"],["rest"]],"dates":["13 Sep","14 Sep","15 Sep","16 Sep","17 Sep","18 Sep","19 Sep"]}]},"educationReferences":{},"anatomyResearch":{"anatomyResearch":{"head":[{"title":"Concussion Return-to-Play Protocols","finding":"Graduated return-to-play over 6 days reduces second-impact risk by 80% vs immediate return.","source":"McCrory et al., Br J Sports Med","year":"2017"},{"title":"Sleep & TBI Recovery","finding":"Athletes sleeping <7 hours post-concussion showed 2.5× longer recovery times.","source":"Silverberg et al., J Neurotrauma","year":"2021"}],"neck":[{"title":"Cervical Strength & Concussion Risk","finding":"Athletes with greater neck strength had 41% lower concussion rates in collision sports.","source":"Collins et al., J Athl Train","year":"2014"},{"title":"Whiplash Fascial Connections","finding":"Deep cervical fascia continuity with thoracolumbar fascia explains persistent neck-back pain chains.","source":"Stecco et al., Clin Anat","year":"2020"}],"shoulder":[{"title":"Rotator Cuff Tendon Loading","finding":"Heavy slow resistance (HSR) training outperforms eccentric-only protocols for tendinopathy at 12 weeks.","source":"Kongsgaard et al., Am J Sports Med","year":"2009"},{"title":"Thoracolumbar Fascia Force Transmission","finding":"Posterior oblique chain (latissimus → opposite glute) transmits ~40% of rotational force through the shoulder.","source":"Vleeming et al., Spine","year":"1995"}],"chest":[{"title":"Breathing & Performance","finding":"Diaphragmatic breathing training improved VO₂max by 3.5% in trained athletes over 8 weeks.","source":"Ferreira et al., J Sports Sci","year":"2016"},{"title":"Rib Stress Fractures in Rowers","finding":"Load management reduced rib stress fracture incidence by 62% in elite rowers.","source":"Christiansen et al., Br J Sports Med","year":"2019"}],"core":[{"title":"Core Stability & Low Back Pain","finding":"Motor control exercises targeting multifidus reduced LBP recurrence from 76% to 35% over 1 year.","source":"Hides et al., Spine","year":"2001"},{"title":"Thoracolumbar Fascia & Back Pain","finding":"Fascial densification at L3-L4 correlates with chronic low back pain severity (r=0.68).","source":"Stecco et al., PLoS One","year":"2013"}],"hips":[{"title":"Hip ROM & Low Back Injury","finding":"Athletes with <35° internal hip rotation had 2.8× higher risk of lumbar injuries.","source":"Vad et al., Am J Sports Med","year":"2004"},{"title":"Glute Strength & Lower Limb Injury","finding":"Weak hip abductors linked to 3× higher rates of IT band syndrome and patellofemoral pain.","source":"Fredericson et al., J Orthop Sports Phys Ther","year":"2000"}],"upperArm":[{"title":"Biceps Tendinopathy Loading","finding":"Eccentric curl protocols reduced pain by 72% at 12 weeks in chronic biceps tendinopathy.","source":"Mellor et al., Clin J Sport Med","year":"2019"}],"elbow":[{"title":"Tennis Elbow Eccentric Loading","finding":"Eccentric wrist extensor training showed 84% improvement vs 53% for corticosteroid injections.","source":"Bisset et al., BMJ","year":"2006"},{"title":"UCL Stress in Throwing Athletes","finding":"Fastball velocity >85 mph exponentially increases UCL strain; pitch counts are the primary modifiable risk factor.","source":"Aguinaldo et al., J Biomech","year":"2007"}],"wrist":[{"title":"Grip Strength & Longevity","finding":"Grip strength is a stronger predictor of all-cause mortality than systolic blood pressure.","source":"Leong et al., Lancet","year":"2015"}],"thigh":[{"title":"Hamstring Strain Eccentric Training","finding":"Nordic hamstring curls reduced hamstring injuries by 51–70% across multiple RCTs.","source":"Petersen et al., Am J Sports Med","year":"2011"},{"title":"Fascial Chain & Hamstring Recurrence","finding":"Posterior line fascial restrictions predict hamstring re-injury better than muscle strength alone.","source":"Stecco et al., Surg Radiol Anat","year":"2019"}],"knee":[{"title":"ACL Rehab Criteria","finding":"Meeting return-to-sport criteria (strength, hop tests) reduced ACL re-injury from 38% to 24%.","source":"Grindem et al., Br J Sports Med","year":"2016"},{"title":"Patellar Tendon Loading","finding":"Heavy slow resistance and isometric holds reduced patellar tendinopathy pain by 60% in-season.","source":"Kongsgaard et al., Am J Sports Med","year":"2009"}],"lowerLeg":[{"title":"Achilles Tendinopathy HSR","finding":"Heavy slow resistance training outperformed eccentric-only loading for chronic Achilles tendinopathy.","source":"Beyer et al., Am J Sports Med","year":"2015"},{"title":"Stress Fracture Load Management","finding":"Tibial stress fractures in runners reduced by 73% when training volume increases stayed below 10%/week.","source":"Nielsen et al., Br J Sports Med","year":"2012"}],"ankle":[{"title":"Ankle Sprain Rehab & Recurrence","finding":"Balance/proprioception training reduced ankle sprain recurrence by 50% in athletes with prior sprains.","source":"McKeon et al., J Athl Train","year":"2008"},{"title":"Plantar Fascia Loading","finding":"High-load strength training outperformed stretching for plantar fasciitis at 3 months.","source":"Rathleff et al., Scand J Med Sci Sports","year":"2015"}]}},"springProgressData":{"paceComparison":{"totalWeeks":12,"anticipated":{"label":"Anticipated plan","reachedStrengthAt":6,"note":"Strength phase from week 6"},"actual":{"label":"Your progress","reachedStrengthAt":5,"note":"Strength phase reached in week 5 — plyometrics already tolerated"},"summary":"~1 week ahead of the anticipated timeline, pain-free, with impact work already underway."},"progressAccordions":[{"title":"What we've done","items":["Settled the early irritation — now pain-free","Completed the protected rebuild phase","Progressed faster than the typical timeline"]},{"title":"What you're doing now","items":["Progressive strength and impact loading","Plyometrics — building spring and control"]},{"title":"What to expect","items":["Strength keeps building over weeks to months","The odd bit of stiffness after harder sessions is normal","Sharp or lasting pain is not — flag it if it happens"]},{"title":"What's next","items":["Rotational and sport-specific loading","Continue progressing impact work","Layering plyometrics toward full court demands"]}],"progressPhases":[{"id":0,"num":"1","label":"Calm it down","sub":"Days 0–5 · Done","state":"done","badge":"Days 0–5","title":"Phase 1 — Calm it down","text":"The early phase, right after the sprain, where we settle the irritation and protect the ligament so healing can start.","list":["Reduce pain and swelling","Protect the arch while the first tissue forms","Gentle movement to keep things mobile"]},{"id":1,"num":"2","label":"Rebuild","sub":"2–6 wks · Done","state":"done","badge":"2 days – 6 weeks","title":"Phase 2 — Rebuild (protected)","text":"New tissue forms, but it's still weak and can be re-injured, so loading stays controlled. You've completed this phase.","list":["Controlled, gradual loading to build the new tissue the right way","Still protected — no full court loads yet","Groundwork for the strength phase"]},{"id":2,"num":"3","label":"Get strong","sub":"6–12 wks · Now","state":"current","badge":"6 – 12 weeks","title":"Phase 3 — Get strong","youAreHere":true,"text":"The tissue slowly turns strong and organised as we load it. This is the longest and most important phase — it's what makes the foot court-ready and protects you from re-injury.","list":["Progressive strength and impact work","Plyometrics (jumping/landing) — already started and tolerated","Keeps building for months — patience here pays off"]},{"id":3,"num":"?","label":"Check-in","sub":"If it flares","state":"check","badge":"Check-in point","title":"If it's not improving","text":"A safety net, not a stage you have to reach. If pain flares or progress stalls, we pause, reassess, and adjust the plan rather than pushing through.","list":["Tell me early if something feels off","We adjust load rather than stop altogether"]}],"progressStatus":{"week":5,"headline":"Week 5 · No pain · Ahead of schedule","detail":"You're in the strengthening phase now, with plyometrics started and tolerated."}},"infographicCategories":{"INFOGRAPHIC_CATEGORIES":[{"id":"movement","label":"Movement & Mechanics","blurb":"How force is produced, absorbed and redirected through the body.","color":"#00E5FF","items":["deceleration","ssc","explosive","shoulder"]},{"id":"training","label":"Training & Adaptation","blurb":"How tissue and performance respond to programmed load over time.","color":"#A3E635","items":["tendon","adaptation"]},{"id":"recovery","label":"Recovery & Longevity","blurb":"Sleep, recovery and the long game of staying capable.","color":"#FBBF24","items":["recovery","sleep","healthspan"]}]},"biomechanicsData":{"BODY_MOTION_IMAGE":"https://media.base44.com/images/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/1f8ebb9bd_generated_image.png","CHAIN_COMPARISON":{"left":{"title":"Open Chain","color":"#fb923c","points":["Distal segment is free to move","Movement occurs at one or few joints","More isolated movement"],"example":"Knee Extension"},"right":{"title":"Closed Chain","color":"#fb923c","points":["Distal segment is fixed","Movement occurs at many joints","More functional, stable movement"],"example":"Squat"}},"EXAM_TIP":"Understand the type of motion to analyse movement patterns, select exercises and prevent injuries.","KEY_POINTS":[{"label":"Motion","text":"Change in position over time"},{"label":"Linear","text":"Straight line"},{"label":"Angular","text":"Around an axis"},{"label":"General","text":"Combination of linear + angular"},{"label":"Human movement","text":"Complex & multi-planar"}],"MOTION_IN_BODY":["Occurs at joints","Produced by muscle contraction","Controlled by the nervous system","Depends on forces acting on the body","Essential for daily activities, sports & rehabilitation"],"MOTION_TYPES":[{"n":1,"title":"Linear (Translatory) Motion","color":"#f472b6","definition":"Every point in the body moves through the same distance in the same direction.","examples":["Sliding a book on a table","Body moving forward in a straight line"],"image":"https://media.base44.com/images/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/df5422570_generated_image.png"},{"n":2,"title":"Angular (Rotary) Motion","color":"#60a5fa","definition":"Body moves around an axis or pivot point.","examples":["Flexion–extension at the elbow","Turning of the head"],"image":"https://media.base44.com/images/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/c6194e9d4_generated_image.png"},{"n":3,"title":"General (Combination) Motion","color":"#4ade80","definition":"Combination of linear and angular movements occurring simultaneously.","examples":["Throwing a ball","Walking / running","Jumping"],"image":"https://media.base44.com/images/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/a81d80629_generated_image.png"}],"TRANSLATION_VS_ROTATION":{"left":{"title":"Translation","color":"#a78bfa","points":["Straight line movement","No change in orientation","All points move equal distance in the same direction"]},"right":{"title":"Rotation","color":"#a78bfa","points":["Movement around an axis","Change in orientation","Points move in circular path around the axis"]}}}};
const ROUTINES = [{"id":"morning","label":"Morning Routine","tag":"ON WAKING","summary":"A 5-step sequence to wake the fascial system before you start your day. Short, gentle, and progressive — from spine to full body.","duration":"5 minutes","steps":[{"title":"Wake Up Your Spine","lead":"Start with gentle spinal movement.","actions":["Reach.","Rotate.","Bend."],"closing":"Your body moves best when your spine moves first.","videoUrl":"https://media.base44.com/videos/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/4fe952d17_Morning_Step_1_-_Spine.mp4"},{"title":"Open Your Hips","lead":"Healthy hips reduce unnecessary stress throughout the rest of your body.","actions":["Move through rotation.","Not just stretching.","Quality over range."],"closing":"Your hips are the engine of every step you'll take today.","videoUrl":"https://media.base44.com/videos/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/38ae154f6_Morning_Step_2_-_Hips.mp4"},{"title":"Restore Shoulder Mobility","lead":"Your shoulders rely on your upper back and ribcage to move efficiently.","actions":["Slow, controlled movement helps restore healthy motion.","Don't rush it."],"closing":"Mobility is built with patience, not force.","videoUrl":"https://media.base44.com/videos/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/4bd4aed43_Morning_Step_3_-_Shoulders.mp4"},{"title":"Wake Up Your Feet","lead":"Your feet are your foundation.","actions":["Mobilize your toes.","Move your ankles.","Reconnect with the ground.","Everything above benefits."],"closing":"Strong arches make a stronger athlete.","videoUrl":"https://media.base44.com/videos/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/a6737648a_Morning_Step_4_-_Feet.mp4"},{"title":"Finish With Full-Body Movement","lead":"Let your entire body move together as one connected system.","actions":["Walk.","Squat.","Reach.","Rotate."],"closing":"Integration is the goal — not isolation.","videoUrl":"https://media.base44.com/videos/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/c7ae29637_Morning_Step_5_-_Full_Body.mp4"}]},{"id":"warmup","label":"Pre-Padel Warmup","tag":"BEFORE PLAY","summary":"A focused activation routine to prime the hips, ankles, and thoracic spine for rotational power and lateral cuts. Run these 5 moves right before stepping on court.","duration":"3–5 minutes","steps":[{"title":"Hip 90/90 Rotation","lead":"Padel is a rotational sport — if your hips can't rotate, your spine takes the load.","actions":["Sit tall.","Rotate side-to-side.","Switch the front leg."],"closing":"Open hips unlock your forehand and bandeja.","videoUrl":"https://media.base44.com/videos/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/77d954db1_Warmup_Step_1_-_Hip_90_90.mp4"},{"title":"World's Greatest Stretch","lead":"Hits hips, hamstrings, thoracic spine, and shoulders in one sequence — the best bang-for-buck mobility drill for padel.","actions":["Lunge deep.","Rotate the torso.","Reach and extend."],"closing":"One move, four fascial lines primed.","videoUrl":"https://media.base44.com/videos/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/784713994_Warmup_Step_2_-_Worlds_Stretch.mp4"},{"title":"Ankle Rockers","lead":"Ankle dorsiflexion is the #1 missing mobility for padel players. Without it, your knees collapse inward on every lateral cut.","actions":["Drive the knee forward.","Keep the heel down.","Track over the toes."],"closing":"Mobile ankles protect your knees.","videoUrl":"https://media.base44.com/videos/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/a89932d55_Warmup_Step_3_-_Ankle_Rockers.mp4"},{"title":"Cat-Cow + Thread the Needle","lead":"Frees the thoracic spine and shoulders locked from desk work and overhead shots.","actions":["Arch.","Round.","Thread and rotate."],"closing":"A free upper back means a heavier smash.","videoUrl":"https://media.base44.com/videos/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/89f4c5215_Warmup_Step_4_-_Thread_Needle.mp4"},{"title":"Deep Squat Hold","lead":"Opens hips, ankles, groin, and lower back all at once — the fastest way to rebuild the athletic base.","actions":["Sit low.","Press the knees out.","Breathe deep."],"closing":"The foundation of every explosive movement.","videoUrl":"https://media.base44.com/videos/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/c1fe645fe_Warmup_Step_5_-_Deep_Squat.mp4"}]},{"id":"evening","label":"Evening Routine","tag":"RECOVERY","summary":"A restorative sequence to decompress, release, and rehydrate the fascial network after a day of play. Slow, controlled, and focused on recovery.","duration":"10 minutes","steps":[{"title":"Spinal Twist Breathing","lead":"The Djokovic method — decompress the thoracic fascia and mobilize the oblique chains through breath-led rotation.","actions":["Inhale and reach.","Twist on the exhale.","Deepen each round."],"closing":"Breath is the original fascial release tool.","videoUrl":"https://media.base44.com/videos/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/11f8e1010_Evening_Step_1_-_Spinal_Twist.mp4"},{"title":"Slow Flow Movements","lead":"Move through unpredictable, winding angles to reach hidden fascial planes and rehydrate the matrix.","actions":["Lengthen.","Spiral.","Breathe."],"closing":"Long, whole-body movements are what fascia craves.","videoUrl":"https://media.base44.com/videos/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/eac2ff71e_Evening_Step_2_-_Slow_Flow_v3.mp4"},{"title":"Soft Tissue Release","lead":"Break up fibrotic adhesions that foam rolling cannot reach, realigning collagen along the lines of tension.","actions":["Scrape along the fibers.","Cross-friction passes.","Realign."],"closing":"Controlled micro-trauma triggers real healing.","videoUrl":"https://media.base44.com/videos/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/f438d7725_Evening_Step_3_-_Soft_Tissue.mp4"},{"title":"Tissue Flossing","lead":"Flush stagnant fluid from deep fascial compartments and flood the matrix with fresh, oxygenated blood.","actions":["Compress.","Move the joint.","Release instantly."],"closing":"Pressure out, circulation in.","videoUrl":"https://media.base44.com/videos/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/f3f57db5a_Evening_Step_4_-_Tissue_Flossing.mp4"},{"title":"Proprioceptive Balance","lead":"Stimulate the dense network of mechanoreceptors housed inside the fascia to rebuild the mind-body connection.","actions":["Balance.","Stabilize.","Reconnect."],"closing":"Recovery is also neurological.","videoUrl":"https://media.base44.com/videos/public/6a8acc7103ac6ef2d4bf2dcc/586f2c069_Evening_Step_5_-_Balance.mp4"}]}];
